The runtime heap size is bounded only by available physical memory, while `FPR_HEAP_MB` serves solely as a cap for testing exhaustion scenarios. The OS reserves address space lazily via `MAP_NORESERVE`, committing pages only when touched, and returns them to the buddy allocator upon freeing blocks of 1 MiB or more. A program cannot exceed the machine's RAM, but a run will panic if the live heap exceeds the value of `FPR_HEAP_MB`.

```fprisc
// Example demonstrating the distinction between physical bounds and the environment cap
// This program allocates memory until it hits the machine limit or the FPR_HEAP_MB cap.
// If FPR_HEAP_MB is 256, the program will panic before exhausting the machine's RAM.
// If FPR_HEAP_MB is set to 64, the program will panic at 64 MiB regardless of available RAM.
let ptr = hal_malloc(1)
while true {
  let size = hal_get_size()
  if size > FPR_HEAP_MB then panic "Heap exhaustion"
  // ... allocate more memory ...
}
```

The heap is now a reservation of address space rather than a fixed size, meaning a program's memory limit is determined by the available machine RAM instead of the `FPR_HEAP_MB` environment variable. While `FPR_HEAP_MB` still caps runs for testing purposes, the honest cost of a leak extends up to the machine's physical memory limit. The runtime manages this via `hal_heap_span` for allocation and `hal_heap_release` for freeing blocks of 1 MiB or more.

```fprisc
// Example demonstrating that a heap can grow beyond the old 256 MiB cap
// limited only by available address space.
let ptr = hal_heap_alloc(1024)
// ... allocate many times ...
// If the machine has enough RAM, this will not panic at 256 MiB.
```

The heap is now a reservation of address space rather than a fixed size, allowing the runtime to commit pages on first touch and release them when blocks of 1 MiB or more are freed. The previous 256 MiB cap (`FPR_HEAP_MB`) served only as a test ceiling for exhaustion scenarios, whereas the new model bounds a program strictly by available memory. This prevents leaks from stopping artificially at 256 MiB; instead, a leak stops only when the machine's address space is exhausted.

```fprisc
// Example demonstrating the new dynamic behavior
let p1 = alloc(); // Commits a page on first touch
let p2 = alloc(); // Commits another page
// No need to manually track size; the system manages the span.
// A leak persists until the OS runs out of address space, not at 256 MiB.
```

The compiler implements an "autodrop" law that automatically discharges Arcs when they are no longer transitively needed by the program's shape, preventing flat Arc growth across thousands of loop iterations. This mechanism ensures that even in complex scenarios involving nested loops, case-shapes, and transitive last-use dependencies, the set of live Arcs remains constant (zero growth) while the total number of round trips accumulates.

