In the context of the `Vec` representation, "amortized O(1)" means that while a specific `push` operation might occasionally trigger a costly memory reallocation (copying data), the cost of this copy is attributed to that specific push operation. Consequently, the average cost per push across a sequence of operations remains constant, ensuring that the Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) remains compositional and predictable.

