QOS Portable is a hosting runtime that executes a single FP-RISC program compiled for a host architecture (such as QOS-x86_64) and packaged as a `.qa` file, satisfying the program's standard assumptions atop the host OS. Unlike QOS Native, which provides real hardware abstractions and schedulers, QOS Portable relies on the host OS for services like libc, sockets, and file systems. This relationship is analogous to a JVM running a `.jar` file or a System.qa running as a loaded process on a virtual machine, where the portable runtime shares the host's address space and boot process mirrors the native stages (Initializer, Loader, user-level QOS) while delegating missing hardware functions to the host.

```fprisc
// Example of a minimal QOS Portable program compiled for a host
// This code assumes the host provides standard I/O and memory management
// as part of the hosting runtime environment.

program hello_world;

    // The runtime handles HAL dispatching; this code runs
    // within the shared address space of the host OS.
    stdout "Hello from QOS Portable\n";

end hello_world;
```

