The raycaster tracks the CELL INDEX rather than absolute coordinates, allowing the loop to use a single `zipLt` comparison against a precomputed `tExit` value. This `tExit` represents the time at which the ray leaves the 16^3 box and is calculated once during setup, ensuring that any ray aliasing across a world edge is already accounted for, thus eliminating the need for six per-axis bounds checks.

```fprisc
# Conceptual loop body showing the single comparison
while (zipLt t tExit) {
  # Advance index using per-lane strides
  idx = zipMul idx, stride;
  idx = zipAdd idx, offset;

  # Fetch world data
  val = gather world, idx;

  # Process val...
}
```

The raycaster optimizes traversal by tracking the CELL INDEX rather than explicit (x, y, z) coordinates, allowing strides of +/-256, +/-16, and +/-1 to be applied via a single zipMul and zipAdd per axis while fetching world data with one gather. Furthermore, it avoids multiple bounds comparisons per iteration by computing a time exit (tExit) once at setup; a lane is removed when tExit is exceeded using a single zipLt operation, ensuring that rays crossing world edges are already handled before entering the loop.
Ax = Type (Ax td tm te ds fid).
tExit = Vec.zipLt tCurrent tExit;
if tExit then exitLane else advance;

Recursive functions are tightly controlled to prevent unbounded resource consumption and stack overflow. The language uses `measure`s to provide a well-founded metric for recursion, ensuring every call progresses toward a base case. This mechanism aligns with the WCET system and other safety features like pattern matching and guards.

```fprisc
measure n = 1

fn fib(n) =
  unsafe {
    match n {
      0 -> 1
      _ -> fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
    }
  }
```

To define a safe recursive function, you must use a `measure` to provide a well-founded metric that ensures each recursive call progresses toward a base case, preventing stack overflow and unbounded resource consumption. This approach aligns with the language's WCET system and is required even when using builtin recursion schemes. Here is an example:
```fprisc
fib(n) = measure(n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    if (n == 1) return 1;
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
}
```

