To create a new session, call the `session` function with the register, poller, application, connection, and request as arguments. If the request fails, it writes a 400 error response and returns `Unit`. On success, it spawns a writer actor, waits for the join result, and extracts the session ID `sid` from the `Welcome` message. If `sid` is 0, no reading function is invoked; otherwise, `reading` is called to establish the session reader.

```fprisc
session reg poller app conn req = case WS.accept req of
    Err why -> _ = ST.write (H.renderResponse (H.text 400 "{why}\n")) conn; Unit
  | Ok head ->
      _ = ST.write head conn;
      me = myself 0;
      writer = A.spawnWith (A.Dynamic 64) (fn self -> writerStart (protocolOf req.query) reg app conn self);
      r = A.askSure me reg (Join writer);
      sid = (case r of Welcome n _ -> n | _ -> 0);
      _ = drop r;
      (if sid == 0 then Unit else reading reg app.codec writer sid (ST.readerOn poller conn) conn A.tidySmall).
```

In FP-RISC, `Vec.filter` performs eager compaction in place, shrinking the vector length and eliminating zero allocations, which ensures subsequent operations like `Vec.map` or `Vec.fold` work on a dense, mask-free vector. To demonstrate this, define a predicate `keepEven` that checks if an integer is even, then apply it to a pre-filled vector. The test verifies that the heap usage remains stable (delta < 4096 bytes) and the resulting vector contains exactly half the original elements.

