The `binCol` function converts a floating-point column into integer codes by first calculating the minimum (`lo`) and maximum (`hi`) values in the column. It then determines the bin width (`w0`) by dividing the range by the number of bins (`nb`), ensuring the width is at least 1.0. Finally, `binGo` iterates through the column, mapping each value to an integer code between 0 and `nb-1` based on its normalized position relative to `lo` and `w`.

```sol
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4).
```

The `binCol` function takes an unsafe Vector of F64 values and the number of bins, returning the resulting codes and updated vectors. It first computes the minimum and maximum values in the column to establish a range, calculates the bin width as the difference between the max and min divided by the number of bins, and then iterates through the data to assign integer codes based on how many widths fit into the difference between each value and the lower bound. If the calculated width is zero or negative, it defaults to 1.0. The final result includes the list of codes, the lower bound, the width, and the modified column vector.

```sol
binCol col nb =
  (n, col1) = Vec.len col;
  inf = 1.0 / 0.0;
  (lo, col2) = Vec.fold fminf inf col1;
  (hi, col3) = Vec.fold fmaxf (0.0 - inf) col2;
  w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
  w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
  (codes, col4) = binGo col3 0 n lo w nb (Vec.new Unit);
  (codes, lo, w, col4).
```

The `binCol` function first calculates the minimum (`lo`) and maximum (`hi`) values of the input column. It then computes the bin width (`w0`) by dividing the range (`hi - lo`) by the number of bins (`nb`). If the resulting width is less than or equal to zero, the function sets the width to `1.0` to ensure valid binning; otherwise, it uses the calculated `w0`. This logic is implemented in the `binGo` helper, which uses this width to map data points to integer codes via `(x - lo) / w`.

```fprisc
w0 = (hi - lo) / F64.ofInt nb;
w = case w0 <= 0.0 of True -> 1.0 | False -> w0;
