The CLAIM-THEN-MASK strategy prevents interrupt storms by having the interrupt drain loop mask a level-triggered source immediately upon claiming it, ensuring the source does not re-pend the interrupt while the actor is servicing it. After the actor completes its work, it must re-arm the source; if the signal is still high, the gateway re-pends the interrupt for a fresh, exactly-once delivery. This mechanism is implemented in `actors.c` via `hal_irq_claim` and `Sys.irqAck`.

```fpr
// Example flow in actors.c
// 1. Claim and mask to prevent re-pending during service
hal_irq_claim(source_id); 

// 2. Actor runs...

// 3. Re-arm after service; if still high, plic re-pends
Sys.irqAck(source_id);
```

The virtual PLIC implements a CLAIM-THEN-MASK discipline to prevent storms. When a drain loop claims a source, it immediately masks the interrupt line to stop re-pending the same event. After the bound actor services the device and calls the acknowledge function, the source is re-armed only if the interrupt line remains high, ensuring exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment.

```fpr
# Example of the CLAIM-THEN-MASK cycle logic
func hal_irq_claim(source_id) -> bool:
    if is_source_enabled(source_id):
        mask_source(source_id)
        return true
    return false

func hal_irq_ack(source_id):
    if is_line_high(source_id):
        unmask_source(source_id)
```

