The `build` function iterates through source text and slot data, injecting HTML span elements with `data-slot` attributes for each slot index. It then populates these spans with the corresponding slot data, reconciles the DOM with the root, and manages focus restoration if the active element is blurred during reconciliation. The `slot` function is a utility that updates the text content of a specific slot element by its key and optionally triggers a visual flash animation by toggling the `lv-flash` CSS class.

```fprisc
function slot(k, txt, flash) \{
    var el = root.querySelector('[data-slot=\"' + k + '\"]');
    if (!el) return;
    el.textContent = txt;
    if (flash) \{ el.classList.remove('lv-flash'); void el.offsetWidth; el.classList.add('lv-flash'); \}
}
```

The `scanLocals` function returns `undefined` (it performs side effects only). It iterates through all elements with the `x-data` attribute, splits that attribute's value by semicolons, and parses each pair to extract variable names and their initial values. These values are then stored in the global `locals` object.

```sol
function scanLocals() \{
    root.querySelectorAll('[x-data]').forEach(function (el) \{
      el.getAttribute('x-data').split(';').forEach(function (pair) \{
        if (!pair) return;
        var eq = pair.indexOf('='), n = pair.slice(0, eq);
        if (!(n in locals)) locals[n] = pair.slice(eq + 1);
      });
    });
  }
```

The `build` function constructs a temporary HTML string by concatenating the source string `S.s` with corresponding `<span>` elements marked by `data-slot` attributes based on the destination array `S.d`. It then parses this string into a new `div`, populates the slots with values from `S.d`, and reconciles the DOM with the root. To handle focus restoration, the function checks if the previously active element is still within the root and has lost focus; if so, it programmatically focuses the original element and re-applies any lost text selection or cursor direction.

```fprisc
var active = document.activeElement;
var start = active && active.selectionStart, end = active && active.selectionEnd;
var direction = active && active.selectionDirection;
reconcile(root, wanted);
if (active && root.contains(active) && document.activeElement !== active) {
  active.focus({preventScroll: true});
  if (typeof start === 'number') active.setSelectionRange(start, end, direction);
}
```

