job start =
  me = myself 0;
  w = spawn (fn self -> jobWait me start self);
  r = receiveFrom me w;
  out = (case r of Ok text -> keep text | Err why -> keep why);
  _ = drop r;
  out.
```

To initiate a Wi-Fi access point scan, call the `Esp.wifiScan` function, passing an integer argument representing the job ID. The function returns an integer job ID that must be passed to `Esp.jobResult` to retrieve the text output. Alternatively, you can use the `job` helper to block execution until the result is ready.

```sol
id = Esp.wifiScan 1;
result = Esp.jobResult id;
```

The `job` function spawns a helper task that calls `Esp.jobNew` to create a job slot, binds the calling actor's interrupt to that slot, and waits for completion. Once the job finishes, the result is retrieved via `Esp.jobResult`, formatted as a string, and returned to the caller.

```sol
job start =
  me = myself 0;
  w = spawn (fn self -> jobWait me start self);
  r = receiveFrom me w;
  out = (case r of Ok text -> keep text | Err why -> keep why);
  _ = drop r;
  out.
```

The function `lblOne` recursively processes a decision tree node by extracting the next elements from the input vectors `xs`, `zs`, and `ns` at index `i+1`. It computes a classification value `c` based on whether the corresponding elements in `xs` and `zs` are positive, then determines the output value `y` using a threshold on `ns`. Finally, it returns a tuple containing the updated vectors `xs2`, `zs2`, `ns2`, and the new output vector `ys` (which includes the computed `y`), along with a recursive call to `lbl` for the next iteration.

```fpr
lblOne i n xs zs ns ys =
  (x, xs2) = Vec.get (i + 1) xs;
  (z, zs2) = Vec.get (i + 1) zs;
  (nz, ns2) = Vec.get (i + 1) ns;
  c = case neqB (x > 0.0) (z > 0.0) of True -> 1 | False -> 0;
  y = case nz < 0.05 of True -> 1 - c | False -> c;
  lbl (i + 1) n xs2 zs2 ns2 (Vec.push y ys).
```

The `lbl` function acts as a recursive case handler that checks if the current index `i` exceeds the limit `n`. If `i >= n`, it returns the remaining vectors; otherwise, it delegates to `lblOne` to process the current node. Inside `lblOne`, `Vec.get` is used to retrieve the next elements from the input vectors (`xs`, `zs`, `ns`) at index `i + 1` to compute the split value `y` for the current level. This retrieval allows the function to consume one element from each vector per recursive step, effectively traversing the tree level by level.

```fprisc
lblOne i n xs zs ns ys =
  (x, xs2) = Vec.get (i + 1) xs;
  (z, zs2) = Vec.get (i + 1) zs;
  (nz, ns2) = Vec.get (i + 1) ns;
  c = case neqB (x > 0.0) (z > 0.0) of True -> 1 | False -> 0;
  y = case nz < 0.05 of True -> 1 - c | False -> c;
  lbl (i + 1) n xs2 zs2 ns2 (Vec.push y ys).
```

