The function iterates through all elements with the `x-data` attribute, splits the attribute value by semicolons to separate key-value pairs, and stores the parsed values in a client-side object.

```sol
function scanLocals() \{
  root.querySelectorAll('[x-data]').forEach(function (el) \{
    var attr = el.getAttribute('x-data');
    attr.split(';').forEach(function (pair) \{
      if (!pair) return;
      var eq = pair.indexOf('=');
      if (eq > 0) \{
        var key = pair.slice(0, eq);
        var val = pair.slice(eq + 1);
        locals[key] = val;
      \}
    \}
    \}
    \}
}
```

The client scans all elements with an `x-data` attribute, splits the attribute value by semicolons, and extracts key-value pairs to populate a local state object. If a variable name is not already defined in the client's locals, it is initialized with the value from the server's declaration.

```fpr
root.querySelectorAll('[x-data]').forEach(function (el) \{
  el.getAttribute('x-data').split(';').forEach(function (pair) \{
    if (!pair) return;
    var eq = pair.indexOf('='), n = pair.slice(0, eq);
    if (!(n in locals)) locals[n] = pair.slice(eq + 1);
  \});
\});
```

The trailing `_` serves as a wildcard to handle any remaining values in an actor loop, ensuring the case is exhaustive without requiring an explicit list of all possible values. This syntax is intentionally accepted even though it might otherwise look like a missing case, because actor loops rely on this pattern to continue execution when no specific condition matches. For example:
