arity = 2;
funVal = funId * 1024 + arity;
args = [arg1, arg2];
```

When `TCP.connect` succeeds, `overTcp` constructs a full HTTP request string by joining the query's method, target, headers, and body, writes it to the connection, reads the entire response, closes the connection, and returns the result of parsing that response. If the write or read fails, it returns an error; otherwise, it returns `parseResponse raw`.

```sol
overTcp q u = case TCP.connect u.host u.port of
    Err why -> Err "{u.host}:{u.port}: {why}"
  | Ok conn ->
      head = strJoin "" (q.method :: " " :: u.target :: " HTTP/1.1\r\nhost: " :: u.host :: "\r\n"
        :: renderHeaders q.headers :: "content-length: " :: str (strlen q.body)
        :: "\r\nconnection: close\r\naccept-encoding: identity\r\n\r\n" :: q.body :: Nil);
      sent = ST.write head conn;
      got = (case sent of Err why -> Err why | Ok _ -> ST.readAll conn);
      _ = ST.close conn;
      (case got of Err why -> Err why | Ok raw -> parseResponse raw).
```

The `parseUrl` function first locates the scheme separator `://` and extracts the scheme, host, and target. If a colon exists in the authority section (host:port), it parses the substring after the colon as an integer; otherwise, it defaults to port 80 for HTTP or 443 for HTTPS. If the port parsing fails or the host is missing, it returns an error.

```fprisc
dflt = (case scheme == "https" of True -> 443 | False -> 80);
port = (case colon == 0 of True -> Ok dflt | False -> S.toInt (S.dropLeft colon authority));
```

