C code does not consume fuel; instead, the fuel model treats every C call as a black box with a fixed duration constant defined by the WCET model. This design allows generated code to spill live state to frame slots before invoking C, ensuring that any blocking or spinning logic must occur outside the fuel budget, typically via the actor machinery (e.g., sending messages or yielding). Consequently, HAL functions must be O(bounded) or explicitly yield to prevent holding the hart indefinitely.

```fprisc
// Example: A HAL function that yields instead of spinning
fn loop_until_done(actor: Actor, cond: bool) -> bool {
