The `claim` function is responsible for claiming a specific size of memory from a block after a split operation has occurred. It initializes the block by marking it as used, clearing its fields and pending list, setting its layout description to null, and calculating the remaining capacity by subtracting 64 words from the block's total size. Finally, it retrieves the payload address and zeroes out the memory region corresponding to the requested allocation size.

```fprisc
claim b size =
  _ = split b size;
  _ = Block.setUsed b (w 1);
  _ = Block.setFields b (w 0);
  _ = Block.setPending b (Block.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setLayout b (Desc.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setCap b (Word.sub (Block.size b) (w 64));
  p = payload b;
  _ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
  p.
```

The `alloc` function first checks for overflow conditions based on the high bits of the requested size. It then calculates the required size by adding an 80-byte minimum and aligning the request upward to a multiple of 8 bytes. If the calculated size is exactly 80 bytes, it is adjusted to 96 bytes to ensure at least one block is available. Finally, it invokes `firstFit` to locate the first suitable block in the free list that is large enough to hold the adjusted size and splits it if necessary.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

