The `spawnUart` function in `system.fpr` handles this by first spawning a `UartM` actor, then executing three `uartCall` operations to perform the open, buffer allocation (8192 bytes), and sharing. It finally returns the actor reference.

```sol
spawnUart me =
  a = spawn UartM.actor;
  r1 = uartCall me a UartM.UOpen;
  r2 = uartCall me a (UartM.UBuf 8192);
  r3 = uartCall me a UartM.UShare;
  a.

uartCall me a msg =
  _ = send a (me, msg);
  r = receiveFrom me a;
  drop r.
```

The `spawnUart` function spawns a `UartM` actor and executes three sequential calls via `uartCall`. In each call, the variables `r1`, `r2`, and `r3` return the result of the `receiveFrom` operation within `uartCall`, which discards the response (`drop r`). The `uartCall` function sends a message from `me` to the actor `a`, receives a reply from `a` back to `me`, and then drops that reply, effectively performing a fire-and-forget RPC to open the UART, allocate a buffer, and share the actor.

```fpr
r1 = uartCall me a UartM.UOpen;
r2 = uartCall me a (UartM.UBuf 8192);
r3 = uartCall me a UartM.UShare;
```

To spawn a UART actor on a specific hart, use the `spawnUart` function which takes the caller's ID (`me`) as an integer argument. Internally, this function spawns the `UartM.actor`, performs a sequence of `uartCall` operations to open the device, allocate a buffer of 8192 bytes, and share the capabilities. The `uartCall` helper function sends a message to the actor and receives a response from the caller's ID, discarding the result.

```sol
spawnUart me =
  a = spawn UartM.actor;
  r1 = uartCall me a UartM.UOpen;
  r2 = uartCall me a (UartM.UBuf 8192);
  r3 = uartCall me a UartM.UShare;
