```

The `doNeq` predicate succeeds if the terms are not unifiable at the moment of execution. It first marks the current state of the store, attempts to unify the two terms, and then undoes those bindings. If unification fails, it proceeds to solve the goal with the original store state; if unification succeeds, it proceeds with the updated store state, effectively asserting inequality.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

