The runtime allows interrupting a blocked poll by opening a watch on the file descriptor, arming it, and then sleeping briefly before releasing the watch. This sequence ensures the kernel raises an interrupt to wake the waiting thread, which subsequently cancels its waiter handles. The following Sol function demonstrates this lifecycle:

```sol
recycle fd n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  idle = need (OS.watchOpen Unit);
  _ = release idle;
  armed = need (OS.watchOpen Unit);
  _ = OS.watchArm armed (fd :: Nil);
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 10000;
  _ = release armed;
  recycle fd (n - 1).
```

The `waiter` function creates a mechanism to interrupt a blocked poll operation by spawning two concurrent waiter threads that monitor a specific file descriptor. It sends a watch notification to a poller, then blocks on a channel to receive a payload indicating whether the watch was cancelled. If the payload is `PL.Cancelled`, the function returns `True`; otherwise, it returns `False`. This allows the system to verify that both spawned threads correctly handle the cancellation signal before the poller is stopped.

```fpr
waiter p fd boss me = case p of PL.Poller a ->
  _ = need (send a (PL.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  cancelled = case note of PL.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = drop note;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.
```

The function `one` returns `Unit`. It spawns two instances of the `waiter` function, receives messages from both, checks that both were cancelled, and then stops the poller before returning.

```sol
one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = PL.stop p;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled".
```

