The dispatch lattice in Sol ensures consistency by defining pure arithmetic functions (like `poly`) that operate on exact `f64` columns, allowing them to execute identically across interpretation, JIT, and GPU tiers provided the input vector meets the minimum size requirement (`n >= SOL_GPU_MIN`). One can generate a large vector of integers using `fill`, map the pure function over it to produce results, and then fold the results to compute a checksum that remains identical regardless of the execution tier.

```sol
poly x = x * x / 7 + x - 3 .
plus a b = a + b .
fill : Vector -> (i : Int | measure (lim - i)) -> (lim : Int) -> Vector .
fill v i lim | i > lim = v.
fill v i lim = fill (Vec.push (Num.sqrt (i * i)) v) (i + 1) lim.

> xs = fill (Vec.new Unit) 1 120000;
  ys = Vec.map poly xs;
  (s, ys2) = Vec.fold plus 0 ys;
  u = print "dispatch: checksum = {s}";
  Vec.free ys2 .
```

