The QOS operating system manages memory slabs for actors by starting with a small initial size and doubling it for each subsequent slab, rather than allocating a fixed large block. This approach prevents excessive initial memory consumption for actors that perform little work. The first slab is now sized as one buddy block (196 MiB), and each subsequent slab is twice the size of the previous one until reaching a maximum of 256 KiB (FPR_SLAB_SZ). Additionally, an actor's first stack is allocated as a single 128 KiB block, which is smaller than the previous 512 KiB implementation.

```sol
// Example of a pool slab configuration logic
// First slab: 196 MiB (one buddy block)
// Subsequent slabs: Double the previous size up to 256 KiB
let first_slab_size = 196 * 1024 * 1024
let max_slab_size = 256 * 1024
let stack_block_size = 128 * 1024
```

The `boxBg` function extracts background (`bgOf`) and border (`bordOf`) attributes from the input, calculating a border inset of one pixel if a border exists. It then renders either an opaque rectangle with the background color or a translucent rectangle with the border color, adjusting the rectangle's dimensions to inset the drawing by the border width.

```sol
boxBg c a x y w h d =
  (has, col) = bgOf a;
  (hasA, colA) = bgaOf a;
  (hasB, colB) = bordOf a;
  i = case hasB of True -> 1 | False -> 0;
  r = roundOf a;
  ri = case r > 0 of True -> r - i | False -> 0;
  case has of
    True -> rrect c (x + i) (y + i) (w - 2 * i) (h - 2 * i) ri d (opaque col)
  | False -> case hasA of
      True -> rrect c (x + i) (y + i) (w - 2 * i) (h - 2 * i) ri d colA
    | False -> c.
```

