The peer actor must implement a mechanism to actively drain or reset its receive queue to prevent the net tier from dropping the session due to the QOS_NET_TXCAP limit of 256 KiB. Without periodic draining, the backlog accumulates until the session is terminated.

```sol
actor Peer {
    let queue: Queue<Message> = new Queue();
    let active: bool = true;

    on msg(m) {
        queue.push(m);
        if (queue.len() > 256 * 1024) {
            active = false; // Trigger cleanup logic here
        }
        // Process messages from the queue
        while (queue.len() > 0) {
            let m = queue.pop();
            handle(m);
        }
    }
}
```

QOS automatically drops a peer's session once its unsent message tail exceeds 256 KiB. While this limit is enforced by the net tier, the peer's actor alone carries the backlog until that threshold is reached. This behavior prevents memory leaks from stalled connections where the remote end ceases to read incoming data.

```sol
// Example demonstrating the stalled consumer behavior
async function test_stalled_peer() {
    let stall = socket.create_connection(("127.0.0.1", PORT), 3);
    stall.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_RCVBUF, 4096);
    handshake(stall);
    stall.sendall(frame(1, b'{"msg":"login","arg":"stalled"}'));

    // Simulate sending large data without reading from the stalled peer
    for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
        stall.sendall(frame(1, b'x'.repeat(3000))); // Send ~3KB, exceeds 256KB over iterations
    }

    // The session will be dropped here due to QOS_NET_TXCAP limit
    stall.close();
}
```

