Omitting the recursive call `shard self cfg next t` causes the function to terminate immediately after processing the current message, preventing the shard from handling subsequent messages or continuing its lifecycle. The correction is to ensure the recursive call is present to process the next state (`next`) and time (`t`), allowing the shard to remain active and responsive.

```fprisc
shard self cfg st0 t0 = case A.tidy self (st0, t0) of (st, t) ->
  m = receive self;
  (from, q) = m;
  (next, ans) = serveShard cfg q st;
  _ = A.sendSure self from ans;
  _ = drop m;
  shard self cfg next t.
```

The `shard` function receives a configuration, a shard state, and a timestamp, then processes the entries by tidying the state and serving the shard via a message loop. It uses `receive` to get a message, `serveShard` to compute the next state and answer, sends the result back using `sendSure`, and recursively calls itself with the updated state.

```fprisc
shard self cfg st0 t0 = case A.tidy self (st0, t0) of (st, t) ->
  m = receive self;
  (from, q) = m;
  (next, ans) = serveShard cfg q st;
  _ = A.sendSure self from ans;
  _ = drop m;
  shard self cfg next t.
```

The `shardStart` function initializes the processing of a specific shard by loading entries from a file path and tidying the resulting data structure before passing it to the `shard` function. The `shard` function then receives this data, interacts with a message queue via `receive` and `sendSure`, and recursively processes the shard until completion.

```sol
shardStart self cfg idx =
