To schedule a delayed message, the actor must send a `TAfter` message containing the desired duration in milliseconds to itself. The QOS timer mechanism calculates the absolute deadline by adding the duration to the current clock value, scaled by `ticksPerMs` (10,000 ticks per millisecond). This deadline is then inserted into a sorted priority queue (`pend`) to ensure messages are processed in order of urgency. If the actor is the main timer loop, the nearest deadline is armed; otherwise, the child actor sleeps until the interval expires.

```fpr
msg = TAfter 500;
send me msg;
```

An actor schedules a future event by sending a `TAfter` message containing the desired duration in milliseconds to the system timer. The timer calculates the absolute deadline by adding the duration to the current clock value, inserts this new deadline into a sorted list of pending events, and arms the nearest deadline. If the current time matches the deadline, the event fires immediately; otherwise, the actor sleeps until the next scheduled poke.

```sol
actor me =
  cl = device "clint";
  clk = reg32 cl 49144;
  nat = Sys.timerBind me;
  loop me clk nat Nil.

loop me clk nat pend =
  m = receive me;
  case m of
    TAfter ms -> doAfter me clk nat pend ms
  | k -> fireDue me clk nat pend.

doAfter me clk nat pend ms =
  dl = read clk + ms * 10000;
  pend2 = insert dl Nil pend;
  if nat == 1 then armNearest clk pend2 else sleepChild me ms;
  fireDue me clk nat pend2.
```

