# The operation itself must be idempotent (e.g., checking existence before write)
# or use a CAS mechanism to ensure only one instance takes effect.
proc.myIdempotentTask = proc {
    if (not exists myKey) {
        create myKey;
        execute sideEffect;
    }
    # If re-run after crash, this block is skipped, ensuring exactly-once effect
    # despite the at-least-once delivery of the process.
}
```

Deferred processes utilize at-least-once crash recovery rather than exactly-once delivery. A durable done marker is recorded after a process returns, meaning a hard crash occurring between the external effect and this marker can trigger a re-run during recovery. To prevent unintended side effects, developers must use idempotent operations, compare-and-swap guards (such as Git `--force-with-lease`), or application-level idempotency keys. No API should be described as rollback-safe for these deferred external processes.

