In the QOS scheduler, the prime hart (hart 0) is designed to handle only timer interrupts and takes no device interrupts unless it is the sole core, whereas device interrupts are routed to auxiliary harts. The timer service operates exclusively on the prime hart to manage CLINT deadlines and wake up blocked actors, while the auxiliary hart responsible for interrupts delivers the actual interrupt signal when due. This separation ensures that the prime hart remains available for critical scheduling tasks like generating ready slots and managing backlogs, while auxiliary harts handle time-sensitive device events.

```fprisc
// Example illustrating the separation:
// The prime hart (hart 0) manages the timer service and scheduling logic.
// The auxiliary hart handles device interrupts via the Plic.

// On the prime hart (hart 0):
// Only timer interrupts are processed here unless hart 0 is the only core.
// Device interrupts are gated off for the prime hart in multi-core setups.

// On an auxiliary hart:
// It wakes up for device interrupts via MEIP and CLINT deadline pops.
// It does not handle the core scheduling logic, which remains on hart 0.
```

