The `fpr_pool_t` type lacks a constructor, requiring manual initialization of five fields at five distinct creation sites; omitting one field causes memory allocations to read stack garbage as freelist heads. In contrast, the `cow_wr` contract mandates that every writing operation on a vector calls `cow_wr` to enforce copy-on-write semantics; failing to invoke it allows in-place compaction to silently mutate and truncate shared views, violating data integrity.

