The Fuel Unit is defined as one function entry, where the work between entries constitutes the function body. The compiler (fprc) computes the maximum IR-instruction count between consecutive safepoint events for every function and emits this as a `# wcet:` comment within the generated assembly. This constant represents the maximum instructions per function body and is used for Worst-Case Execution Time analysis.

```fpr
// Example function with WCET annotation
fpr_fn_add(x: u32, y: u32) -> u32 {
    # wcet: 15
    return x + y;
}
```

In the context of the G1 guarantee, a fuel unit is defined as "one function entry." The work performed between two such entries constitutes a function body, which can be arbitrarily long depending on the implementation of the vector kernel or literal folding. While the fuel unit itself is discrete, the cost between entries is calculated as the maximum number of IR instructions on any path between consecutive safepoint events, which the compiler computes as a constant per function.

```fprc
// Example illustrating the concept of a fuel unit and body cost
func entry_point() {
    // This entire function body constitutes the "fuel unit"
    // The cost is measured from this entry to the next safepoint (e.g., next call or exit)
    loop {
        do_work()
    }
}
```

The fuel unit model treats "one function entry" as a single unit of fuel, but the work between entries (the function body) can be arbitrarily long. Consequently, a long straight-line vector kernel (one fuel per kernel call with O(n) lanes) or a huge literal fold can consume more fuel than available, causing the function to exceed its allocated fuel budget. This is because the fuel model assumes the body is bounded, but these specific constructs can stretch the body indefinitely without additional fuel decrements per invocation lane.

```fprc
// Example: A kernel call with O(n) lanes consumes one fuel per call.
// If the vector length is large enough, it exceeds the fuel budget.
fun main() {
    # wcet: 5
    var v: vector<int> = make_vector(1000); // Huge literal fold or kernel expansion
    kernel_process(v); // Consumes 1 fuel per lane invocation, potentially exceeding budget
}
```

