> h2 = writeAll h "written transactionally\n";
> close h2.

These lines perform explicit linear-handle discipline to safely write a string to a file. The first line opens the file and rebinds the handle to `h`. The second line uses `writeAll` to write the string to the handle, rebinding the handle to `h2` (which is the same underlying file resource). Finally, `close h2` consumes the handle, ensuring the file is closed and the write is committed to disk. This sequence guarantees that the file is opened exactly once, written exactly once, and closed exactly once, preventing resource leaks and ensuring transactional integrity.

