To trigger a QEMU virt CLINT software interrupt, the `fire` function is called with a counter `n`. Internally, this function resets the interrupt flag, sets the Machine Interrupt Pending (MSIP) register to 1, waits for the flag to be cleared by the CPU, and then recursively calls itself with `n-1` to fire the interrupt multiple times. Verification is performed by the `check` function, which asserts that the counter increments sequentially from `n` to `n+1`, ensuring the interrupt resumed correctly before proceeding.

```sol
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

The `fire` function generates a sequence of software interrupts by writing to the machine interrupt pending register (MSIP) and an interrupt flag, then waits for the flag to clear to confirm the interrupt was handled. It accepts a count `n` and recursively fires `n` times, verifying that the interrupt resumes exactly when expected.
```sol
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

This expression returns the updated value of the interrupt flag register after incrementing it by one. In the FP-RISC Sol profile, `Mem.readWord flag` retrieves the current 32-bit integer stored at the address `flag` (0x80000000), and `Word.fromInt 1` creates a word containing the integer 1. The `Word.add` function then computes their sum, resulting in a `Word` value that reflects the incremented state of the flag, which serves as the mechanism to signal that the interrupt has been processed and the system should resume.

```sol
flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
_ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.add (Mem.readWord flag) (Word.fromInt 1));
```

