The FP-RISC loader reads the LOAD section's text directly, performing a hand-rolled key scanner over the decimal values to ensure consistency, followed by a copy operation. This mechanism is utilized by the portable host, plugins, and the native kernel to load applications and processes, with the `qosp.fpr` component also scanning the section for the `sha` line.

```fprisc
// Example of scanning a LOAD section key in FP-RISC
let key = scan_load_section_text();
if (key is_valid) {
    copy_load_section(key);
}
```

The LOAD section in FP-RISC is read directly by the FP-RISC runtime rather than being handled solely by a C loader, necessitating a hand-rolled scanner to parse the `key <decimal>` format and enforce consistency rules before copying. This approach ensures that all callers, including the portable host loading apps or plugins and the native kernel loading processes, execute FP-RISC logic on their side. The implementation in `runtime/qaimg.c` demonstrates this with a loop that scans the text section:
```fprisc
# Scan LOAD section for key <decimal> pairs
while (text_ptr < end_ptr) {
    key = scan_decimal(text_ptr);
    if (key == 0) break;
    if (validate_consistency(key)) {
        copy_section(key);
    }
}
```

To invoke the load path, you must call a hand-rolled `key <decimal>` scanner that operates over the LOAD section's text, followed by a copy operation. This sequence is executed by the portable host when loading an application or plugin, and by the native kernel when loading a process, while `qosp.fpr` performs a second scan for the `sha` line.

```sol
// Example invoking the load path logic
key_scan = load_section_text;
key_scan(key = decimal_value);
copy_operation();
```

`runtime/qaimg.c` handles the initial, hand-rolled scanning of the LOAD section's text, performing five consistency checks before copying the image. In contrast, `qosp.fpr` scans the same section a second time specifically to locate the `sha` line. Both components operate within the FP-RISC environment, with `qaimg.c` managing the portable host loading logic for apps, plugins, and processes, while `qosp.fpr` executes the subsequent verification step.

```fprisc
// Example of the scanning logic in qaimg.c
key = scan_load_section_text();
if (consistency_checks(key) == 5) {
    copy_image(key);
}
```

The file `fprisc/examples/logbook.fpr` implements a server-side script that populates form inputs by name when data arrives via the `live:set` event, ensuring values are applied even if the event precedes the DOM element's creation. It utilizes a pending object to store incoming values and a MutationObserver to detect when input elements appear in the document, at which point it assigns the stored values and triggers native `input` events to update the UI state.

```sol
script = strcat (LJ.src 0) "
(function () {
  var pending = {};
  function fill() {
    Object.keys(pending).forEach(function (n) {
      var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-input="' + n + '"]');
      if (!els.length) return;
      els.forEach(function (el) { el.value = pending[n]; el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); });
      delete pending[n];
    });
  }
  document.addEventListener('live:set', function (e) {
    var vals = JSON.parse(e.detail);
    Object.keys(vals).forEach(function (n) { pending[n] = vals[n]; });
    fill();
  });
  new MutationObserver(fill).observe(document.getElementById('lv-root'), { childList: true, subtree: true });
})();
".
```

The `fill` function synchronizes pending data values with their corresponding DOM elements by iterating over the `pending` object and locating elements with the matching `data-input` attribute. It assigns the stored value to the element's `value` property and triggers an `input` event to notify the server that the value has been updated. This mechanism ensures that values are populated even if the `live:set` event arrives before the DOM element is rendered, as the MutationObserver re-runs `fill` whenever the target document is modified.

```sol
function fill() {
  Object.keys(pending).forEach(function (n) {
    var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-input="' + n + '"]');
    if (!els.length) return;
    els.forEach(function (el) { el.value = pending[n]; el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); });
    delete pending[n];
  });
}
```

The `fill` function iterates over the keys of the `pending` object to locate all DOM elements matching the specific data attribute `data-input`. For each element found, it sets the element's value to the corresponding pending data and dispatches a native 'input' event to trigger UI updates. If no matching elements exist for a key, the function skips that key.

```sol
fill() {
  Object.keys(pending).forEach(function (n) {
    var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-input="' + n + '"]');
    if (!els.length) return;
    els.forEach(function (el) { el.value = pending[n]; el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); });
    delete pending[n];
  });
}
