The scheduler maintains three distinct queues to manage task execution across decentralized harts. The runqueue holds tasks currently being executed or ready to run, limited to a fixed size N. The ready queue contains tasks that are ready to run but are waiting for their turn within the runqueue. The backlog queue stores tasks that are pending because a specific condition has not yet been met before they can be promoted to the ready queue.

```sol
// Example of a task moving from backlog to ready based on a condition
task myTask {
    precondition = false;
    when precondition == true {
        move to ready;
    }
}
```

The scheduler operates as a decentralized mesh where no single global entity exists; instead, each hart or executable processor manages its own local scheduling decisions and communicates with other harts to coordinate execution. Each hart maintains three specific queues: a runqueue of size N for tasks currently executing or ready, a ready queue for tasks waiting for their turn, and a backlog queue for tasks pending conditions to move to the ready state.

```sol
// Conceptual representation of a hart's queue management
struct HartState {
    runqueue: Queue<Task>
    ready: Queue<Task>
    backlog: Queue<Task>
}

// Each hart processes its own local queues and coordinates via IPC
func hart_process(state: HartState) {
    if (state.runqueue.is_empty()) {
        state.ready.move_next_to_runqueue()
    }
    // Backlog tasks wait for conditions before moving to ready
}
```

