The `ALIGN(0x10000)` directive ensures a gap between the read-execute (r-x) and read-write (rw) segments, which enforces the W^X (write XOR execute) security boundary. This alignment allows the loader to round the executable size (`execsz`) up to the nearest page size; if this rounded size exceeds the write-protected offset (`rwoff`), the loader must refuse the load to prevent writing into executable memory.

```sol
# Example of the alignment constraint logic
if (execsz_rounded > rwoff) {
    reject_load();
}
```

This alignment ensures that the `execsz` value (the executable size) is honest and does not exceed the `rwoff` (read-write-only) boundary. If a loader were to round `execsz` up to a page size, the alignment prevents this from reaching the `rw` segment, allowing the loader to safely refuse such a configuration. This mechanism enforces the W^X (write-executable-to-non-writable) security principle.

```sol
// Example of the alignment constraint in a linker script
r-x {
    ALIGN(0x10000)
}
rw {
    /* rwoff starts here, execsz must not round up to touch this */
}
```

