In this context, "nondeterministic" refers to the ability of the model checker to explore multiple possible execution paths, such as different orders of free operations or arbitrary input values, to verify that the code holds true for all scenarios. For example, the text describes a test case with "a nondeterministic free order" where the tool explores various sequences to ensure safety invariants are met.

```sol
// Conceptual example of nondeterminism in a loop
// The tool tries different values for 'i' or 'j' automatically
for (i in 0..25) {
  for (j in 0..25) {
    // Verify invariant holds for this specific path
    assert(buddy_invariant(i, j));
  }
}
```

Property-based testing using Hypothesis is preferred because it can generate and shrink complex, long-running sequences of allocation and deallocation operations to verify invariants like non-overlapping blocks and payload integrity over hundreds of steps. In contrast, bounded model checking with CBMC struggles with scalability; for instance, a test with three nondeterministic frees generated thousands of verification conditions and crashed with a memory limit of 16 GB, whereas property-based tests successfully validated 300 sequences of up to 60 steps in 5.7 seconds. While CBMC is useful for single operations or parsers with specific unwind sets, property-based testing is necessary to cover entire sessions.

```sol
// Example of a property-based test sequence logic in Sol
// This draws alloc/realloc/free sequences and checks invariants after every step
for step = 1 to 60 {
  if (random() < 0.5) {
    alloc(request_size);
  } else if (random() < 0.5) {
    free(last_block);
  }
  // Check: live blocks never overlap
  // Check: every payload byte-pattern intact
  // Check: free bytes + live bytes <= arena size
}
