The `accPoll` function serves as the main polling loop for the acceptor actor, responsible for processing incoming network connections and managing the pool of active connections. It iterates through available connection slots, reading data from each active connection using `netRead`. Upon receiving data, it spawns a new actor via `actorFor` to handle that specific connection and immediately sends the received data to it. The function then checks the length of the received bytes; if the length is zero, it marks the connection slot as free (removing the actor) and increments the slot counter, otherwise it retains the connection. This process repeats until all 64 slots are filled, at which point the actor transitions to a boundary state and resets the loop.

```sol
accPoll me st cid =
  As reg port cfg app map k ltt mt tickMs = st;
  bytes = netRead cid;
  (a, map2) = actorFor reg port cfg app map cid k;
  _ = send a (Data bytes);
  map3 = case strlen bytes == 0 of True -> mDel map2 cid | False -> map2;
  accNext me (As reg port cfg app map3 (k + 1) ltt mt tickMs).
```

The `accPoll` function is used within the `acceptor` actor to manage incoming network connections. It takes the current state, a connection ID, and an initial counter of 0. If the counter is 0, it enters an idle state; otherwise, it reads data from the specified connection ID using `netRead`, spawns a new actor to handle the data, sends the received bytes to that actor, and updates the connection map accordingly. If the data length is zero, the connection is removed from the map; otherwise, the connection remains active.

```sol
accPoll me st cid =
  As reg port cfg app map k ltt mt tickMs = st;
  bytes = netRead cid;
  (a, map2) = actorFor reg port cfg app map cid k;
  _ = send a (Data bytes);
  map3 = case strlen bytes == 0 of True -> mDel map2 cid | False -> map2;
  accNext me (As reg port cfg app map3 (k + 1) ltt mt tickMs).
```

