To demonstrate parallel execution, spawn multiple instances of a CPU-bound function (like Fibonacci) on different cores using `spawnOn`, then collect results via a message-passing loop. The `timed` function illustrates this by distributing `jobs` instances of `fib n` across available harts, timing the execution, and aggregating the core assignments and results received from each worker.

```sol
timed me label jobs n =
  t0 = Esp.ms Unit;
  _ = List.each (fn k -> spawnOn (k % Sys.harts Unit) (worker me k n)) (List.range 0 (jobs - 1));
  lines = collect me jobs Nil;
  ms = Esp.ms Unit - t0;
  _ = print "{label}: {jobs} x fib {n} in {ms} ms";
  ms.
```

The `worker` function spawns an actor on a specific CPU core to calculate the Fibonacci number `n` and report the core IDs where it executed. It begins by calling `Esp.core Unit` to obtain the identifier of the core currently running the function. It then calculates the Fibonacci value using the recursive `fib` definition. Finally, it sends a record containing the worker ID, the two core identifiers (the current one and a default one), and the computed Fibonacci value to the `boss` actor via `send`. This mechanism allows the program to verify which core handled the computation.

```fpr
worker boss k n me =
  c0 = Esp.core Unit;
  v = fib n;
  _ = send boss (R k c0 (Esp.core Unit) v);
  0.
```

The `timed` function measures the duration of a task by recording the start time, spawning a list of jobs on designated cores, collecting results, and calculating the elapsed time in milliseconds. It requires four arguments: a message channel (`me`), a label for the output string, a list of target core indices (`jobs`), and the number of Fibonacci iterations (`n`). The function spawns each job on the specified core using modulo arithmetic, waits for all results via `collect`, and prints the total time taken.

```fpr
t0 = Esp.ms Unit;
_ = List.each (fn k -> spawnOn (k % Sys.harts Unit) (worker me k n)) (List.range 0 (jobs - 1));
lines = collect me jobs Nil;
ms = Esp.ms Unit - t0;
_ = print "{label}: {jobs} x fib {n} in {ms} ms";
ms.
```

