Historically, `qa_t.perms` was a fixed 32-entry structure where permissions exceeding this limit were silently dropped, while `MAX_GRANTS` was a static 64-entry ledger that was never read by the application. Both approaches failed to grow with demand. The current implementation replaces the fixed `qa_t.perms` with a heap-allocated array that doubles in size to accommodate any number of permissions, and removes the `MAX_GRANTS` ledger entirely, relying instead on shared-buddy slabs that are reaped with their associated ACBs.

```sol
