The file `fprisc/sol/lib/logic.sol` implements the core logic for executing Sol programs within the QOS operating system, specifically handling instruction execution, clause matching, and fuel management. It defines `tryClauses` to iteratively attempt program clauses until success or failure, `tcStep` to execute a single instruction step by unifying memory addresses and managing variable allocation, and `tcBody` to process the body of a clause. The module culminates in `runQuery`, which initializes the execution environment, sets up the goal stack, invokes the solving engine, and returns the final solutions in order along with the remaining fuel.

```sol
runQuery db syms goalsPT nqv qpairs fuel =
  hp = Vec.new Unit;
  tr = Vec.new Unit;
  m0 = (hp, 1, 0, tr, 1, 0);
  m1 = allocVars nqv m0;
  (m2, gaddrs) = instArgs goalsPT 1 m1;
  (m3, fuelLeft, sols) = solveG db syms m2 gaddrs fuel qpairs [];
  (hp2, ht, hl, tr2, tt, tl) = m3;
  u1 = Vec.free hp2;
  u2 = Vec.free tr2;
  (rev [] sols, fuelLeft).
```

The `tcStep` function extracts the instruction head, body, and new variables from the current clause, allocates memory for the new variables, and executes the instruction to generate a new memory state and fuel. It then attempts to unify the instruction's heap address with the provided goal address; if successful, it proceeds to execute the instruction body, deducts one fuel unit, and recursively calls `tryClauses` with the updated state. If unification fails, the instruction is skipped without consuming fuel.

```sol
tcStep db syms m c rest g gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hd, body, nv) = c;
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (hp0, vbase0, hl0, tr0, tt0, tl0) = m;
  m1 = allocVars nv m;
  (m2, haddr) = inst hd vbase0 m1;
  (m3, ok) = unify haddr g m2;
  (m4, fuel2, sols2) = tcBody db syms m3 ok body vbase0 gs (fuel - 1) qvars sols;
  m5 = undoTo hm tm m4;
  tryClauses db syms m5 rest g gs fuel2 qvars sols2.
```

