The `one` function spawns two identical waiter processes and waits for both to complete before issuing the stop command. It verifies the correct cancellation order by checking that the first waiter received a cancellation signal while the second did not, ensuring the stop contract is met before any new registrations can occur.

```sol
one p fd me =
  a = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  b = spawn (waiter p fd me);
  _ = receiveFrom me a;
  _ = receiveFrom me b;
  _ = P.stop p;
  _ = check (receiveFrom me a) "first waiter not cancelled";
  check (receiveFrom me b) "second waiter not cancelled".
```

The `waiter` function initiates a registration process for a file descriptor by sending a `Watch` message to the poller and a registration message to a boss process, then waits for a response. It distinguishes between a successful registration (returning `Unit`) and a cancellation (returning `True`), while ignoring other responses. This ensures that the poller only stops after confirming both the poller and the boss have acknowledged the registration, preventing race conditions where new registrations occur after a stop is announced.

```sol
waiter p fd boss me = case p of P.Poller a ->
  _ = need (send a (P.Watch fd me));
  _ = need (send boss me);
  note = receiveFrom me a;
  cancelled = case note of P.Cancelled -> True | _ -> False;
  _ = drop note;
  _ = need (send boss cancelled);
  Unit.
```

The `cycles` function recursively executes a defined number of poller start and stop operations for a given file descriptor and identity. It takes three arguments: the file descriptor (`fd`), the identity (`me`), and the number of remaining cycles (`n`). If `n` is zero, it returns `Unit`; otherwise, it starts a poller, spawns a waiter process to register with the file descriptor, stops the poller, and then recurses with `n` decremented by one.

```sol
cycles fd me n = if n == 0 then Unit else
  p = P.start Unit;
  _ = one p fd me;
  q = P.start Unit;
  _ = P.stop q;
  cycles fd me (n - 1).
```

