The previous implementation of `buddy_reserve_range` allocated memory by fencing off the 1 GiB window into 64 KiB units and writing a free-list node into each one, which caused excessive memory usage and OOM kills. The new design avoids this by taking the range as whole aligned blocks and writing nothing inside it, as implemented in `runtime/buddy.c`. This approach prevents the overhead of populating free-list nodes for every small unit, improving performance and stability.

```sol
// Example of the new allocation strategy where no internal nodes are written
buddy_reserve_range(addr, size)
  // Allocate the range as a whole aligned block without internal node writes
  return new_block(addr, size)
```

The buddy allocator is used to reserve specific ranges of memory, such as a 1 GiB window, by writing free-list nodes into 64 KiB units, whereas Transparent Huge Pages automatically round the first write in an arena up to a 2 MiB page. This distinction affects memory footprint significantly; enabling THP with `madvise` reduces the application's resident memory from 224 MiB to 86 MiB on Ubuntu, while the standard buddy allocator implementation previously caused a 1 GiB machine to OOM-kill the application due to excessive memory usage.

```sol
// Example of memory allocation strategy difference
// Standard buddy allocation (inefficient for large contiguous needs)
buddy_reserve_range(1 GiB, 64 KiB) -> {
    // Writes 16,384 free-list nodes, causing high memory pressure
}

// THP allocation (efficient for large contiguous needs)
madvise(arena, 2 MiB, THP_MADVISE) -> {
    // Single write rounds up to 2 MiB page, reducing overhead
}
```

The file `fprisc/sol/examples/linreg.sol` implements linear regression using full-batch gradient descent on the FP-RISC Numeric datatype. It generates synthetic data following the model $y = 1.75x_1 - 0.5x_2 + 0.25 + \text{noise}$, where features and weights are inexact Numerics, and computes the gradients for weights and bias by summing residuals weighted by features across the dataset. The code leverages the Sol scripting profile to perform these calculations natively in unboxed f64 SoA columns, with the JIT compiler specializing the fold operations based on weight types.

```sol
# Example: Generating a single data row with noise
mkRow s =
  s1 = Rand.next s; s2 = Rand.next s1; s3 = Rand.next s2;
  x1 = Rand.unit s1; x2 = Rand.unit s2;
  noise = Rand.gauss4 s3 / 20;
