The file `runtime/apps_data.c` serves as a diskless fallback mechanism, providing direct access to the app registry when applications are not loaded from the persistent `disk.img` log. It is generated by `tools/genapps.py` and allows the system to reach apps through `Apps.*` symbols without relying on the storage actor or the `apps/index` record.

```c
// Example usage of the diskless fallback registry
void app_launch(const char* app_id) {
    // Directly access the app entry without reading disk.img
    AppsEntry* entry = Apps_get(app_id);
    if (entry && entry->entry_symbol) {
        fpr_fn_main(entry->entry_symbol);
    }
}
```

The system discovers installed applications by reading an `apps/index` record from the disk log, which lists all available `.qa` archives. Upon launch, the `System.qa` actor reads each `.qa` file on demand through the same storage actor used for key-value streams; these archives contain a MANIFEST section with the app ID and entry symbol, and an ELF section with the bundled binary. If disk access fails, the system falls back to the rodata registry, which holds a diskless copy of the apps generated by `tools/genapps.py`.

```sol
# Example of reading an app index and manifest (conceptual Sol logic)
read "apps/index" -> idx;
foreach app in idx do:
    read app -> qa_record;
    parse qa_record.manifest -> meta;
    launch meta.entry_symbol;
end;
```

