The file `fprisc/tests/eq.fpr` serves as a comprehensive test suite for the equality operator (`==`) in the FP-RISC functional language. It validates that structural comparison correctly handles various data types, including strings, algebraic data types (constructors with payloads), nested structures, deep recursion with cycle guards, and records. The tests confirm that two values are equal only if they share the same structure and all corresponding leaf nodes are identical, while differing in any field or leaf results in inequality.

```sol
# Example of a record equality test from the suite
_ = yes "records by field" ({ a = 1, b = "x" } == { a = 1, b = "x" });
_ = no "records differ by field" ({ a = 1, b = "x" } == { a = 1, b = "y" });
```

The `==` operator in FP-RISC performs structural equality by recursively comparing the tag of each value and then comparing their payloads. If the tags differ, the values are unequal; if the tags match, the function recurses into the payloads until a difference is found or an empty structure is reached.

```sol
fn eq(x, y) {
  if (x == y) return yes;
  if (x.tag != y.tag) return no;
  if (x.isList && y.isList) {
    if (x.len != y.len) return no;
    for (i = 0; i < x.len; i++) {
      if (!eq(x[i], y[i])) return no;
    }
    return yes;
  }
  if (x.isTuple && y.isTuple) {
    if (x.len != y.len) return no;
    for (i = 0; i < x.len; i++) {
      if (!eq(x[i], y[i])) return no;
    }
    return yes;
  }
  return no;
}
```

The equality operator `==` returns `yes` only if both terms are structurally identical, meaning they must share the same constructor (variant) and have matching payloads at every level of nesting. If the constructors differ, the payloads differ, or any leaf value within a nested structure differs, the result is `no`. This structural comparison recurses all the way down to the innermost elements; for instance, two lists of the same length are equal only if every corresponding element is equal, regardless of depth. However, the system imposes a cycle guard at depth 64, causing comparisons of extremely deep structures to return `yes` if they match up to that point, effectively treating lists differing only beyond the 65th element as equal.

```fprisc
_ = yes "65-element lists, same" (longList 64 7 == longList 64 7);
_ = no "65-element lists differing only in the last element" (longList 64 7 == longList 64 8);
```

