The `alloc` function validates that the requested size fits within a 64-bit word, aligns the size to an 80-byte boundary (minimum 96 bytes), and delegates the actual block search and reservation to `firstFit`. If a suitable free block is found, `claim` modifies the block's metadata to mark it as used, sets its layout and capacity, and initializes its payload with zeros.

```fpr
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `alloc` function takes a `Word` representing the desired number of bytes and returns an `Addr` pointing to the allocated memory. It first checks for overflow conditions based on bits 62 and 63, aligns the requested size to 80-byte boundaries (or 96 bytes if the result is exactly 80), and then performs a first-fit search starting from the head of the free list to find a suitable block.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

