The `scene2d.fpr` module defines the core rendering logic for the 2D scene system within the QOS operating system, specifically handling the creation of geometric primitives and text output. It provides high-level functions like `boxBord` for drawing rectangular borders with rounded corners and `emitText` for rendering text based on a 3x5 pixel font grid. These functions rely on lower-level mesh generation utilities such as `ring`, `strips`, and `arcEnt` to construct the visual elements, which are then processed by the QOS graphics pipeline.

```sol
emitText c t x y sc col d = etGo c t 1 (strlen t) x y (sc, col, d);
etGo c t i n x y st | i > n = c;
etGo c t i n x y st =
  (sc, col, d) = st;
  c1 = glyph c (font (charAt t i)) x y sc col d;
  etGo c1 t (i + 1) n (x + 6 * sc) y st;
```

To render a glyph, you must first retrieve the 15-bit pattern for the character from the font table using its index, then iterate through the bits to emit `arcEnt` primitives. Each bit represents a quarter-circle segment; if set, an arc is drawn at the calculated position and depth. The function calculates the center coordinates based on the current x/y, the segment size (3 pixels), and the depth, then emits the arc with the appropriate direction and color.

```sol
emitGlyph c idx x y st =
  (sc, col, d) = st;
  (font, bits) = fontOf c;
  charIdx = charAt font idx;
  glyphBits = bitsOf charIdx;
  cx = x;
  cy = y;
  sz = 3;
  i = 0;
  while i < 15 do
    bit = (glyphBits div 2^i) mod 2;
    if bit = 1 then
      arcEnt c cx cy sz (0 - 1) 1 d col
    else
      pass
    fi;
    i = i + 1;
  end;
  c.
```

In Phase 6, `Vec.filter` evaluates the predicate branchlessly using cmov or SIMD lanes to write a mask column, avoiding per-element taken/not-taken branches. Scans like map, fold, and zip fuse with this mask by reading it as a lane select, while density is restored either by an explicit `Vec.compact` call or automatically at `Sys.poolReset`. This approach prioritizes a fused, branch-light path over eager in-place compaction.

```sol
// Example of the fused mask usage in a scan operation
let mask = Vec.filter(vec, predicate)
let result = Vec.scan(mask, initial, combine)
```

The Phase 6 strategy replaces per-element branching with a branch-light predicate evaluation that writes a mask column, which is then fused with scan operations via lane selection. This inverts the previous preference for eager in-place compaction to keep scans mask-free; instead, density is restored later by an explicit `Vec.compact` call or at a `Sys.poolReset` frame boundary.

```fprisc
// Phase 6: Filter writes a mask column instead of compacting in place.
// The mask is read by scans as a lane select.
Vec.filter(input, predicate) {
    mask = evaluate_predicate_branchlessly(input, predicate); // cmov/SIMD lanes
    output = scan_fuse_mask(mask, input);
}

// Density is restored at the loop boundary.
Vec.compact(output);
```

The design proposes `Map` as the canonical collection name to avoid introducing a different collection model and to provide convenient literals for string-keyed maps, such as those used for environment variables and simple textual configuration. While `Map` serves as the standard for flat string-keyed data, nested or heterogeneous data is handled via the `Value` type, which uses `Map String Value` to preserve types before decoding into typed records.

