If `argv` contains more than one element, the function correctly returns an error message stating "usage: shell.sol DIR". To fix the code to prevent this error and ensure it only accepts a directory name, the pattern matching must explicitly handle the case where the list has more than one element before the single-element case. The corrected code should use `List.len argv == 1` to guard the directory execution, or check `List.len argv > 1` to trigger the error immediately.

```sol
execute argv = case argv of
    dir :: [] -> (code, out) = sh "ls -l {dir}";
                 ls = List.filter big (Str.lines out);
                 u = map (fn l -> print "{sizeOf l} {nameOf l}") ls;
                 print "{List.len ls} entries, exit {code}"
  | _ -> error "usage: shell.sol DIR".
```

The fix ensures that `argv` is a list of length 1 (e.g., `["DIR"]`) before attempting to execute the shell command. The original pattern `dir :: []` matches any non-empty list, including lists with multiple elements, which causes the error to be raised only after the shell command is incorrectly executed.

You can invoke the `shell.sol` script by passing the directory path as an argument to the `execute` function, which internally runs `ls -l` and parses the output using `Str.parse` and `Str.words` to extract file sizes and names. Below is a complete example that lists all files in the current directory:

```sol
import sol/scripts/text/shell.sol;

execute (args Unit) = {
    dir = ".";
    (code, out) = sh "ls -l {dir}";
    ls = List.filter (fn l -> List.len (Str.words l) >= 9) (Str.lines out);
    u = map (fn l -> Str.parse (Str.words l ! 5) + " " + Str.words l ! 9) ls;
    print "Files: {List.len u}";
    for l in u do print l;
