Upon successfully accepting a WebSocket request, the `session` actor spawns a dynamic `writer` actor to handle outgoing messages. It then asks itself to join the writer's task to retrieve a session ID (`sid`) from the welcome message. If the `sid` is non-zero, the actor initiates a reading session by calling `reading`, passing the register, codec, writer, session ID, and connection details to the `ST` actor.

```fprisc
writer = A.spawnWith (A.Dynamic 64) (fn self -> writerStart (protocolOf req.query) reg app conn self);
r = A.askSure me reg (Join writer);
sid = (case r of Welcome n _ -> n | _ -> 0);
(if sid == 0 then Unit else reading reg app.codec writer sid (ST.readerOn poller conn) conn A.tidySmall)
```

When an actor receives a WebSocket request via `WS.accept`, it checks for errors and writes a 400 response if one occurs. If the request is successful, the actor spawns a dynamic writer actor using `A.spawnWith`, then asks its own register for the join result to obtain the session ID. The actor then either executes a reader function or returns `Unit` if the session ID is zero.

```fprisc
session reg poller app conn req = case WS.accept req of
    Err why -> _ = ST.write (H.renderResponse (H.text 400 "{why}\n")) conn; Unit
  | Ok head ->
      _ = ST.write head conn;
      me = myself 0;
      writer = A.spawnWith (A.Dynamic 64) (fn self -> writerStart (protocolOf req.query) reg app conn self);
      r = A.askSure me reg (Join writer);
      sid = (case r of Welcome n _ -> n | _ -> 0);
      _ = drop r;
      (if sid == 0 then Unit else reading reg app.codec writer sid (ST.readerOn poller conn) conn A.tidySmall)
```

When a request is accepted, the system spawns a dynamic writer actor with a 64-unit stack to handle the specific protocol. It then queries the register to join this writer and retrieves a session ID from the resulting `Welcome` message, defaulting to 0 if the join fails. If a valid session ID is assigned, the system attaches a reader to the existing poller connection to manage subsequent I/O for that session.

```fprisc
session reg poller app conn req =
  case WS.accept req of
    Err why -> ST.write (H.renderResponse (H.text 400 "{why}\n")) conn; Unit
  | Ok head ->
      _ = ST.write head conn;
      me = myself 0;
      writer = A.spawnWith (A.Dynamic 64) (fn self -> writerStart (protocolOf req.query) reg app conn self);
      r = A.askSure me reg (Join writer);
      sid = (case r of Welcome n _ -> n | _ -> 0);
      _ = drop r;
      (if sid == 0 then Unit else reading reg app.codec writer sid (ST.readerOn poller conn) conn A.tidySmall)
```

`Vec.filter` performs eager in-place compaction within the same vector, shrinking its length without allocating new heap memory. This ensures that subsequent operations like `Vec.map` or `Vec.fold` work on a dense, mask-free vector. For example, filtering a vector of 1000 integers keeps only 500 elements while maintaining zero allocation:

```sol
a1 = fillI (Vec.new Unit) 1 1000;
h0 = heapUsed Unit;
a2 = Vec.filter keepEven a1;
h1 = heapUsed Unit;
```

Yes, `Vec.filter` performs eager, in-place compaction where kept rows slide down within the same vector, shrinking the length without zero allocation. This ensures that every subsequent operation, such as `Vec.map`, remains dense and mask-free.

```fprisc
v = unsafe Vector;
h0 = heapUsed Unit;
a = Vec.filter keepEven (fillI v 1 1000);
h1 = heapUsed Unit;
-- Verify in-place compaction (h1 - h0 < 4096)
-- Verify density after map
s = Vec.fold (fn a x -> a + x) 0 (Vec.map (fn x -> x * 2) a);
```

The `expand` function iterates through the current frontier, attempting to add each tile's valid neighbors to the frontier and distance list using the `try` function. It processes neighbors in four cardinal directions (up, down, left, right), reusing the results of previous checks to avoid redundant calculations. If a neighbor is blocked or already visited, it is skipped; otherwise, it is added to the accumulator and the distance list.

```fprisc
expand m ((x, y) :: r) dist d acc =
  (a1, d1) = try m (x - 1) y dist d acc;
  (a2, d2) = try m (x + 1) y d1 d a1;
  (a3, d3) = try m x (y - 1) d2 d a2;
  (a4, d4) = try m x (y + 1) d3 d a3;
  expand m r d4 d a4.
```

The compiler will report an "uninitialized variable" error because `b` is used in the expression `acc * 2 + b` without being declared. In FP-RISC, every variable must be explicitly typed and initialized before use. The fix is to add a type declaration and initialization for `b` immediately before its assignment: `b = pinR sdo;` should be preceded by `b : bit = 0;` (or simply `b : bit = pinR sdo;` if the language allows implicit initialization for that specific scope, but explicit typing is required). The corrected line sequence is:
```sol
b : bit = 0;
b = pinR sdo;
```

The TTP229 chip is not a matrix; it scans itself internally. The host drives the SCL line and samples the SDO line after every falling edge of SCL to read one bit per key, starting with the MSB-ordered key 1. To read a single bit, the host must drive SCL low to trigger a chip shift, sample SDO for the bit value, then drive SCL high to return to the idle state before the next read cycle.

```fpr
rmBit pinW pinR scl sdo i acc =
  _ = pinW scl 0;
  b = pinR sdo;
  _ = pinW scl 1;
  rmGo pinW pinR scl sdo (i + 1) (acc * 2 + b).
```

