Because each connection owns its own actor, a slow or hostile peer stalls only that specific actor. The system continues to scale with memory rather than a table, as new sessions are spawned independently and do not block the processing of other connections. The register still broadcasts the new model to all sessions, but the stalled actor simply fails to render or push its delta until it recovers or is closed.

```sol
// Example of a connection actor that handles its own state
actor ConnectionActor {
  let buffer = Buffer.new();
  let stats = Stats.new();

  onReceive(self, msg) {
    if msg == "data" {
      buffer.append(msg);
      // Parse frames and send events to register
      register.sendEvent(parsedFrame);
    }
    if msg == "close" {
      closeQueue.post(connectionId);
    }
  }
}
```

Every actor automatically receives the frame boundary from `std.mvu` each turn as a message containing state to update itself. To prevent loop-based garbage collection, the actor reads the state back using the `keep` command. The state pool is reset via the `Sys.poolReset` command.

```sol
actor conn {
  // Receive frame boundary state from std.mvu each turn
  receive StdMvuFrameBoundary(state) {
    // Update internal state
    update(state)

    // Read state back to ensure it persists without loop garbage
    keep(state)
  }

  // Reset the state pool when needed
  on Sys.poolReset {
    resetPool()
  }
}
```

