When a `FPut` operation is received, the server calculates the shard index by dividing the key by the configuration span. It then retrieves or spawns the corresponding shard, asks it to perform the store operation, and counts the resulting entries. Finally, it updates the shard counts map, aggregates the total count, and sends a completion signal to the client.

```fprisc
serveFront self from q st = case st of Fs cfg shards counts -> (case q of
    FPut x ->
      idx = (cfg.key x) / cfg.span;
      (sh, shards2) = (case M.get idx shards of
          O.Some s -> (s, shards)
        | O.None -> s = spawnShard cfg idx; (s, M.insert idx s shards));
      r = A.askSure self sh (SPut x);
      n = (case r of SCountIs c -> c | _ -> 0);
      _ = drop r;
      counts2 = M.insert idx n counts;
      _ = A.sendSure self from (FDone (total counts2));
      Fs cfg shards2 counts2)
```

When the shard index is missing, the function spawns a new shard using `spawnShard`, inserts it into the map, and then proceeds to execute the `SPut` operation on that newly created shard.

```fprisc
idx = (cfg.key x) / cfg.span;
(sh, shards2) = (case M.get idx shards of
    O.Some s -> (s, shards)
  | O.None -> s = spawnShard cfg idx; (s, M.insert idx s shards));
r = A.askSure self sh (SPut x);
```

The fuel accounting for vector kernels is invocation-based, where a `Vec.map` over n elements consumes exactly one fuel unit regardless of the element count, treating the O(n) work as a single atomic cost. In contrast, clause fallthroughs in a logic group consume fuel linearly based on the number of clauses traversed; falling through k clauses costs exactly k fuel units with O(1) stack usage, as the fallthrough is compiled into a saturated tail call to a lifted supercombinator.

```sol
// Example: Vector kernel consumes 1 unit for 100 iterations
let result = Vec.map [1..100] (\x x+1) in
  // 1 fuel unit consumed for the entire map operation

// Example: Clause fallthrough consumes 1 unit per clause
let outcome = case (x) of
  | true  -> 1
  | false -> 0
  | _     -> 0
in
  // 3 fuel units consumed for falling through 3 clauses
```

To configure the baud rate, the system must first enable the divisor latch access by writing the value 131 to register 3. Once enabled, the lower 8 bits of the divisor are written to register 0, and the upper 8 bits are written to register 1. Finally, register 3 must be written with 3 to disable the divisor latch and restore the 8N1 configuration. This sequence is implemented in the `doParams2` function:

```fprisc
_ = write (reg8 dev 3) 131;
_ = write (reg8 dev 0) (band d 255);
_ = write (reg8 dev 1) (BITSHIFTR d 8);
_ = write (reg8 dev 3) 3;
```

To configure the UART baud rate, the owner must first enable the Divisor Latch Access Bit (DLAB) by writing 131 (0x83) to register 3, then write the low byte of the divisor to register 0 and the high byte to register 1. Finally, restore DLAB to 3 to complete the 8N1 configuration.

```fpr
doParams me st from d =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = write (reg8 dev 3) 131;
  _ = write (reg8 dev 0) (band d 255);
  _ = write (reg8 dev 1) (BITSHIFTR d 8);
  _ = write (reg8 dev 3) 3;
  reply me from UOk st.
```

A user must first invoke `doOpen` with the device address, their process ID as the owner, and the FIFO size to claim exclusive ownership of the device. Only if the owner check passes within `doOpen` can they subsequently call `doParams` with the same owner ID and the desired divisor value to configure the baud rate.

```sol
doOpen 0x00 myPid 64;
doParams 0x00 myPid 115200;
```

