When an actor in the backlog has waited longer than the configured threshold `tau` (defined by `FPR_TAU`), the scheduler enters the aged tier and admits the oldest such actor first, ensuring deterministic behavior without randomness. This mechanism guarantees that no actor waits indefinitely beyond the admission bound `g_tau`.

```fpr
if (g_adm - ready_at > FPR_TAU) {
    admit_oldest_aged_actor();
}
```

The OLDEST such actor is admitted first. The `g_adm` counter is the machine-wide admission counter stamped on every READY actor upon entry to the backlog.

```sol
-- Example of checking the age condition in runtime/core/actors.c
if (g_adm - ready_at > g_tau) {
    // Select oldest actor from backlog
    next_actor = backlog_oldest();
}
```

When a backlog actor has waited more than `tau` admissions (calculated as `g_adm - ready_at > g_tau`), the scheduler admits the OLDEST such actor first in a deterministic, non-random manner. This behavior is part of the "Aged tier" of the two-tier bounded-latency work-stealing scheduler, which is tunable via `schedSetTau` with a default value of `FPR_TAU = 64`.

```fpr
actor main() {
  // Simulate a backlog actor waiting for admission
  // The scheduler will admit this actor if its wait time exceeds FPR_TAU (64)
  // based on the current admission counter g_adm.
  while (true) {
    schedPick(); // Triggers admission logic: Aged tier checks g_adm - ready_at > g_tau
    // Work here...
  }
}
```

`g_adm` is the machine-wide admission counter stamped on every READY actor when it enters the backlog. It serves as the reference point for the Aged tier of admission, allowing the scheduler to determine if an actor has waited longer than the configured threshold `tau` by comparing its admission time (`ready_at`) against the current counter.

```fpr
// Example of how g_adm is used in the admission logic
if (g_adm - ready_at > g_tau) {
    admit_oldest(); // Deterministic admission based on wait time
}
```

