The `render` function toggles a `code` flag when encountering lines starting with triple backticks. When `code` is true, it accumulates lines into a buffer `buf` and, upon seeing a closing block, wraps the joined buffer in HTML `<pre>` tags after escaping special characters using the `esc` helper.

```fpr
if (raw.startsWith('```')) \{
  if (code) \{ out.push('<pre>' + esc(buf.join('\\n')) + '</pre>'); buf = []; }
  code = !code; continue;
\}
```

The error is that the `esc` function is called on the entire string `s` before the inline formatting regexes are applied, causing any formatting markers like asterisks or backticks to be converted into HTML entities (e.g., `*` becomes `&*`) before the code replaces them with tags. This results in malformed HTML where formatting tags contain escaped characters instead of the intended symbols. To fix this, the `esc` function should only be applied to the final output string after all replacements are complete, rather than at the start of the `inl` function.

```fpr
const inl = s => esc(s).replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code class="inline">$1</code>')
                      .replace(/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g, '<b>$1</b>');
```

Corrected version:
```fpr
const inl = s => s.replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, '<code class="inline">$1</code>')
                      .replace(/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g, '<b>$1</b>');
return esc(inl(s));
```

