Reference counting (Rc) is strictly confined to intra-actor usage and cannot cross actor boundaries. If a program incorrectly attempts to share reference-counted data across actors, the Rc mechanism will not function as intended because the inc/dec operations are plain stores that do not synchronize across boundaries. This can lead to memory leaks or double-frees if the reference count reaches zero in a way the single-actor protocol assumes, or if the data is deallocated while still being accessed by another actor.

```fprisc
// Incorrect: Attempting to share Rc data across actor boundaries
actor A {
    let shared_data = Rc::new(10);
}

actor B {
    // This will not correctly share the Rc data as intended
    let shared_data = A.shared_data; 
}
```

In the linear regime, bulk data grows via realloc-by-doubling and is released all-at-once at its single consume point via functions like `Vec.free` or scope exit. For reference-counted data, the `dealloc` command is invoked explicitly when the reference count reaches zero to free the memory.

```sol
// Linear growth and deallocation example
let vec = Vec.alloc(size);
vec.push(item); // Triggers realloc-by-doubling if capacity exceeded
// ... usage ...
Vec.free(vec); // All-at-once release at consume point

// Rc zero deallocation example
let node = Rc.alloc(Node::new());
// ... usage ...
Rc.dec(node); // If count hits zero, dealloc is called
```

