```fprisc
frameWorker parent self =
  message = receiveFrom self parent;
  Frame x z keys buttons yaw pitch = message;
  _ = drop message;
  Input nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch =
    pollInput (Input keys buttons yaw pitch) 64;
  (nextX, nextZ) = move x z nextKeys;
  _ = renderFrame nextX nextZ nextYaw nextPitch;
  reply = Frame nextX nextZ nextKeys nextButtons nextYaw nextPitch;
  _ = send parent reply;
  _ = drop reply;
  _ = Sys.poolReset 0;
  frameWorker parent self.
```

The `fill` function recursively constructs a vector by invoking `mkRow` for each element, which generates synthetic data points using Gaussian random numbers for features `x1` and `x2`. The true label `ytrue` is determined by a nested-rectangle rule based on these features, and a 5% label noise rate is applied by randomly flipping the label if a uniform random number falls below 20. The resulting vectors contain exact integer labels (`+1` or `-1`) while feature comparisons against thresholds utilize the inexact numeric tier.

```sol
mkRow s =
  sa = Rand.next s;
  x1 = Rand.gauss4 sa;
  sb = Rand.next4 sa;
  x2 = Rand.gauss4 sb;
  sc = Rand.next4 sb;
  flip = Rand.uniform sc 20; # 5% label noise
  ytrue = case x1 > quarter of
    False -> 0 - 1
  | True -> (case x2 > minusHalf of True -> 1 | False -> 0 - 1);
  {x1 = x1, x2 = x2, y = case flip == 0 of True -> 0 - ytrue | False -> ytrue}.
```

The `mkRow` function generates a single data instance by first advancing the random state with `Rand.next`, then sampling two features using Gaussian distributions (`Rand.gauss4`). It calculates a true label `ytrue` based on a nested-rectangle rule where `y` is +1 if `x1 > 0.25` AND `x2 > -0.5`. To simulate 5% label noise, it generates a random flip probability and inverts the label with a 1 in 20 chance, ensuring the final label `y` differs from `ytrue` only in the noisy cases.

```sol
mkRow s =
  sa = Rand.next s;
  x1 = Rand.gauss4 sa;
  sb = Rand.next4 sa;
  x2 = Rand.gauss4 sb;
  sc = Rand.next4 sb;
  flip = Rand.uniform sc 20;
  ytrue = case x1 > quarter of
    False -> 0 - 1
  | True -> (case x2 > minusHalf of True -> 1 | False -> 0 - 1);
  {x1 = x1, x2 = x2, y = case flip == 0 of True -> 0 - ytrue | False -> ytrue}.
```

In portable mode, `T.after` does not fire immediately; instead, it spawns a sleeper child that will wake up after the specified duration. Consequently, `receiveFrom` will block indefinitely because no message is currently available in the mailbox, as the `T.after` event has not yet been scheduled to fire. To fix this, the code must explicitly wait for the deadline to fire before attempting to receive the message. This is typically done by spawning a separate actor to handle the `T.after` event or using a mechanism that synchronizes the wait with the deadline, such as `T.wait` or ensuring the `T.after` actor processes its queue before the main actor proceeds.

Corrected pattern (conceptual):
```fpr
# Instead of receiving immediately, wait for the deadline to fire
# before attempting to receive the message.
_ = T.after svc 20;
# Explicitly wait for the deadline event to be processed
_ = T.wait svc; 
f = receiveFrom me svc;
```

In the FP-RISC QOS operating system, a fire-and-forget timer is triggered by `T.after`, which schedules the event and returns immediately without blocking the caller. To verify delivery, the calling process must use `receiveFrom` to poll its own mailbox for the `TFire` message. If the message is successfully received as `TFire`, the event was delivered correctly to the caller's context; otherwise, it indicates a failure or misconfiguration.

```sol
main =
  me = myself 0;
  svc = spawn TM.actor;
  T.after svc 20;
  f = receiveFrom me svc;
  aok = case f of TFire -> "ok" | o -> "WRONG";
```

The `spawn` keyword creates a new actor process that runs concurrently with the current one. In this example, `spawn TM.actor` launches the timer actor defined in the `TM` module, assigning it the unique identifier `svc`. This allows the main actor to interact with the spawned timer actor via message passing, such as using `T.ping svc` to check its status or `T.after svc 20` to schedule a future event.

```fpr
