The FP-RISC language utilizes explicit feature boundaries and automatic ARC (Automatic Region Control) mechanisms to eliminate unnecessary stack frames during tail calls. When a function is called as the final operation within a loop or recursion, the compiler can recognize this pattern and remove the current stack frame, effectively fusing the call into the loop body. This optimization ensures that no live allocations occur for the tail-called function, allowing the program to run efficiently with minimal memory overhead. For example, a loop defined in FP-RISC that calls a helper function at the end of its iteration will have that helper's stack frame discarded immediately after execution, resulting in a single, optimized loop construct rather than a series of nested function calls.

