The script uses a transactional model where all mutating tool calls, such as `mkdirp` and `writePath`, are buffered and applied atomically only at the end of the script. This guarantees that nothing touches the disk until the entire sequence validates successfully, preventing any mid-script failures from leaving a half-done mess.

```sol
u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n\ngenerated by sol\n";
u3 = writePath "{root}/src/main.sol" "> print \"hello from {base.baseName root}\".\n";
u4 = writePath "{root}/test/basic.sol" "> print \"test stub\".\n";
```

The script fails because mutable operations (like `mkdirp`) must be queued before any read-only operations (like `use`) that depend on them; defining `u0` and `u1` after the `use` statement causes the filesystem changes to be applied too late or ignored. The fix is to move the `mkdirp` calls to the top of the script, before the `use` statement, so they are buffered and applied atomically with the subsequent writes.

```sol
scaffold root =
  u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
  u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
  base = use "../lib/base";
  u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n\ngenerated by sol\n";
  u3 = writePath "{root}/src/main.sol" "> print \"hello from {base.baseName root}\".\n";
  u4 = writePath "{root}/test/basic.sol" "> print \"test stub\".\n";
  print "scaffolded {root} (4 files, 2 dirs) — atomic".
```

