Local state is client-only and never round-trips to the server unless an event explicitly requests it. The client scans elements with an `x-data` attribute to initialize a local state object, and sends current values to the server only when triggered by an event handler.
```fpr
locals = Object.create(null);
root.querySelectorAll('[x-data]').forEach(function (el) {
  el.getAttribute('x-data').split(';').forEach(function (pair) {
    if (!pair) return;
    var eq = pair.indexOf('=');
    if (!(pair.slice(0, eq) in locals)) {
      locals[pair.slice(0, eq)] = pair.slice(eq + 1);
    }
  });
});
```

The client identifies dynamic slots by their `data-slot` attribute index and updates the text content of the corresponding `<span>` element without performing a virtual DOM diff. You construct the HTML string by interleaving static segments (`S.s`) and dynamic segments (`S.d`), inserting the span only if a dynamic value exists for that index.

```fpr
var out = '';
for (var i = 0; i < S.s.length; i++) {
  out += S.s[i];
  if (i < S.d.length) out += '<span data-slot="' + i + '">' + S.d[i] + '</span>';
}
root.innerHTML = out;
```

The client initializes local state by scanning all elements with the `x-data` attribute, parsing the semicolon-separated key-value pairs, and storing them in a client-only `locals` object. This process occurs after the initial DOM construction and before any further updates, ensuring the client knows the initial values for its own state management without round-tripping to the server.

```sol
function scanLocals() {
  root.querySelectorAll('[x-data]').forEach(function (el) {
    el.getAttribute('x-data').split(';').forEach(function (pair) {
      if (!pair) return;
      var eq = pair.indexOf('='), n = pair.slice(0, eq);
      if (!(n in locals)) locals[n] = pair.slice(eq + 1);
    });
  });
}
```

The file `qos/docs/SCHEDULER.txt` documents the cooperative, non-preemptive scheduling strategy employed by the QOS operating system, which relies on random selection for basic scheduling or Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) thresholds when Active Control Blocks (ACBs) exceed limits. The implementation offloads device interrupts and timer services to auxiliary harts, ensuring the prime hart (0) remains free for critical tasks unless it is the sole core. This is achieved by binding the timer service actor to the CLINT driver, which arms the nearest deadline and triggers an interrupt on the last live hart, allowing blocked callers to transition from backlog to ready queues via ordinary message wake-ups.

```sol
Sys.timerBind = bindActor("services/timer", "timer.qa");
Sys.timerArm = armDeadline(nearest(pendingSet));
```

On a single-core machine, the prime hart (hart 0) is configured to take no device interrupts unless it is the only core. In this specific case, the system allows the prime hart to handle device interrupts. This behavior is enforced by the `fpr_irq_hart` logic in `actors.c`, where the Plic enables interrupts only on the last live hart. If there is only one core, that core becomes the interrupt handler, preventing the need for an auxiliary hart to drain interrupts.

```fprisc
// Example of the condition logic derived from the reference
if (num_cores == 1) {
    fpr_irq_hart = 0; // Prime hart handles interrupts
} else {
    fpr_irq_hart = last_live_hart; // Auxiliary hart handles interrupts
}
```

On a single-core system, the prime hart (hart 0) does not receive device interrupts; instead, all device interrupts are routed to an auxiliary hart. To prevent deadlock on single-hart machines, the system employs a hart-0 deadlock detector that samples every 30 milliseconds. If no timer interrupt occurs within this window, the detector's deadline overrides the standard `mtimecmp` arm, ensuring the hart remains active and responsive.

```fprisc
// Example of a timer client using the system timer service
Sys.timerBind(self, Sys.timerArm);
Sys.timerArm(1000); // Arms the nearest deadline in milliseconds
```

