The `==` operator implements a cycle guard that terminates comparison after reaching a depth of 64. If two structures are identical up to that point, the operator returns `yes` immediately, treating them as equal regardless of differences in deeper levels. This behavior is demonstrated by comparing two lists of 100,000 elements where only the final element differs; the comparison returns `no` because the difference occurs before the depth limit is reached, whereas two identical lists of the same length return `yes`.

```fprisc
_ = no "100000-element lists differing only in the last element" (longList 99999 7 == longList 99999 8);
_ = yes "100000-element lists, same" (longList 99999 7 == longList 99999 7);
```

The `==` operator performs a deep structural comparison, recursively checking every field and element from the outermost container down to the innermost leaf. It returns `yes` only if the structures are identical in shape and content at every level; any difference at any depth results in `no`. This recursion is limited by a cycle guard at depth 64 to prevent infinite loops, meaning lists differing only beyond the 64th element are treated as equal.

```fprisc
_ = yes "nested, same" (mkNest 1 == mkNest 1);
_ = no "nested, one leaf differs" (mkNest 1 == mkNest 2);
```

