The `doNeq` predicate succeeds if the terms are not unifiable at the moment of execution, which is defined as the bindings being undone. It achieves this by first recording the marks of the term using `marks`, attempting to unify the terms, and then undoing those marks with `undoTo` before checking the unification result. If unification fails, the predicate proceeds to solve the goal with the modified term state; otherwise, it returns the modified term without solving.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

The `doNeq` predicate first marks the current state of the environment, then attempts to unify the two terms. If unification succeeds, the predicate undoes those bindings and returns false (indicating they are equal). If unification fails, it returns true (indicating they are not equal), effectively succeeding iff the terms are not unifiable right now.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

The `doNeq` predicate succeeds if and only if two terms are not unifiable at the current moment. It achieves this by first recording the current variable bindings using `marks m` to obtain `hm` and `tm`. It then attempts to unify the terms with `unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m`. If unification fails (the `ok` case is `False`), the predicate immediately returns success by solving the goal. If unification succeeds, the code uses `undoTo hm tm m1` to restore the environment to its state before the unification attempt, effectively undoing any temporary bindings. This ensures that the predicate returns true only when the terms cannot be made equal, regardless of whether a temporary unification path existed.

```sol
doNeq db syms m f gs fuel qvars sols =
  (hm, tm) = marks m;
  (m1, ok) = unify (f + 1) (f + 2) m;
  m2 = undoTo hm tm m1;
  case ok of
    True -> (m2, fuel, sols)
  | False -> solveG db syms m2 gs fuel qvars sols.
```

