The FPRC compiler calculates WCET by computing the maximum IR-instruction count on any path between consecutive safepoint events (such as entry checks, kernel checks, or calls) for each emitted function. This is achieved by treating the emitted assembly as a Control Flow Graph (CFG) which is specifically a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) because all loops are implemented as tail calls. Consequently, if a back-edge existed, the compiler would report the execution as UNBOUNDED, ensuring that no function has an unbounded execution path within the model. The resulting counts are emitted as `# wcet:` comments per function before lowering, representing the maximum instructions between safepoints rather than hardware cycles.

```fprc
// Example: A function with a tail-recursive loop is transformed into
// a straight-line sequence, allowing FPRC to compute a finite WCET.
// Without this tail-call optimization, a loop would create a back-edge,
// triggering the UNBOUNDED detection described in G1.

fprc
  fn loop_until_done {
    if cond then
      goto loop_until_done // Tail call, no back-edge in CFG
    else
      ret
    end
  }
end
```

In the FPRISC model, a fuel unit is defined as a single function entry, whereas a safepoint event represents any point where execution can be interrupted or checked, such as entry checks, kernel checks, or calls into `fpr_fn_*` functions. The work executed between two consecutive fuel units constitutes a function body, and the fuel unit model relies on calculating the maximum number of IR instructions between these safepoints to determine worst-case execution time (WCET).

```fprc
# Example of a safepoint event in FPRISC code
func entry_check {
    # This function entry is a fuel unit and a safepoint event
    # The work here counts towards the WCET calculation
    load reg, 0x10
    add reg, reg, 1
}

func kernel_loop {
    # This is a function body between two safepoints
    # If this function is called, its instruction count adds to the fuel unit cost
    loop {
        add reg, reg, 1
        break if reg > 100
    }
}
```

