Exceeding the `ARC_CAP` limit triggers a panic because the system treats the capacity as a hard stated limit rather than a degradable state. The worst-case execution time (WCET) for ring operations is determined solely by the message count and lock hold times (increment/decrement under lock), not by data size or allocation overhead. Attempting to send a message when the ring is full results in an immediate panic, as the model enforces this boundary strictly.

```fpr
// Example of a panic condition when ARC_CAP is reached
if (arc_count >= ARC_CAP) {
    panic("table-full");
}
```

The worst-case execution time for ring buffer operations is determined solely by the number of messages sent or received, not by memory allocation or data size. Specifically, the cost is calculated as the sum of sends multiplied by the `incref-under-lock` cost plus received messages multiplied by the `decref-under-lock` cost. The associated lock hold time corresponds to a bounded probe of a fixed-size table defined by `ARC_CAP`; exceeding this capacity triggers a panic rather than performance degradation. This end-to-end behavior is validated by the ring test, which requires the model to remain live after 100k promote/drop cycles.

```sol
// Example cost calculation logic
let total_wcet = (sends * incref_cost) + (received * decref_cost);
```

