Files are treated as a higher-level concept where a URL maps to a unique ID. To retrieve the file's data, the system folds all chunks associated with that specific ID together. This recursive chunk-based structure ensures that every component, including driver code, is managed as a file that can be loaded into memory as executable code for a service actor.

```fprisc
# Example: Retrieving data for a file identified by "my_service"
let chunks = Disk.query("my_service")
let fileData = Disk.fold(chunks)
```

In the FP-RISC disk subsystem, a chunk is defined by a header containing metadata and a body containing data, with both sections combined to form a hash. This recursive structure serves as the foundational data unit for the system.

```sol
chunk {
    header = { ... };
    body = { ... };
}
```

The Disk Subsystem utilizes a recursive chunk-based system where data is organized into headers and bodies with hashes, treating "files" as higher-level concepts that map URLs to IDs and are assembled by folding chunks. In contrast, the Scheduler is inherently a decentralized mesh topology with no global scheduler; instead, each hart maintains its own local scheduling decisions using three specific queues: a runqueue for currently executing or ready tasks, a ready queue for tasks waiting for a turn, and a backlog queue for tasks awaiting conditions to be met.

```fprisc
// Disk Subsystem Concept
chunk = {
  header: { id: "file_001", hash: "abc123" },
  body: { data: [0x00, 0x01, 0x02] }
}

// Scheduler Concept (Local Hart State)
hart_state = {
  runqueue: [task_a, task_b], // Size N, executing or ready
  ready: [task_c],            // Waiting for turn
  backlog: [task_d]           // Waiting for condition
}
```

The file `fprisc/std/live.fpr` implements a live-reloading mechanism that monitors a list of file paths for changes. When a file is detected as modified, it triggers a rebuild process using a provided script configuration. If the rebuild succeeds, the updated program is served to clients; otherwise, the system continues serving the old version and logs the failure details. This functionality is managed via a `watcher` function that sleeps briefly between checks and handles the state transitions of watching, rebuilding, and responding to reload requests.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

The `watcher` function operates in a loop, sleeping for 300 seconds before checking the modification time and size of files in the `watch` list against the `seen` record. If the `stamps` function returns a new list of `(path, timestamp)` pairs compared to `seen`, the function logs an error to standard error and executes the `rebuild` script. If the rebuild succeeds with a return code of 0, it sends a `Reload` signal to the client; otherwise, it continues serving the old program.

```fprisc
watcher reg cfg seen self =
  _ = Sys.sleepUs 300000;
  now = stamps cfg.watch;
  (if now == seen then watcher reg cfg seen self
   else
     _ = Sys.stderr "live: a source changed; rebuilding\n";
     (case P.run cfg.rebuild of
         Ok r -> (if r.status == 0 then _ = A.sendSure self reg (0, Reload); Unit
                  else _ = Sys.stderr "live: the rebuild FAILED; still serving the old program\n{r.out}{r.err}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)
       | Err why -> _ = Sys.stderr "live: cannot run the rebuild: {why}\n"; watcher reg cfg now self)).
```

The file `qos/tools/qainfo.fpr` serves as a base program for the POSIX system that interprets `.qa` manifest files, providing a portable mechanism to read and display application capabilities. It utilizes the `manifest` module to extract metadata such as the application name, ID, load mode, ABI, and shell, while also counting and listing required and optional permissions. When executed with the `--caps` flag, it outputs the full capability blob granted to the application, effectively bridging the gap between the kernel's launch code and the application's declared permissions.

