The four acceleration tiers are specialized performance layers built atop a deliberately pessimized interpreter to maximize speed, whereas the interpreter serves as the foundational execution engine. This architectural choice prioritizes raw performance over code simplicity, as the interpreter is intentionally less optimized to allow the acceleration tiers to handle the majority of execution efficiently.

```sol
-- The interpreter (simplified conceptual representation)
-- Intentionally less optimized to allow acceleration tiers to dominate

fn interpret_expr(expr: Expr) -> Result<Value, Error> {
    // Generic, unoptimized dispatch
    match expr {
        Expr::Add(l, r) => { /* slow generic add */ },
        Expr::Mul(l, r) => { /* slow generic mul */ },
        // ...
    }
}

-- The acceleration tiers (simplified conceptual representation)
-- Highly specialized, optimized for specific expression patterns

fn vec_accelerate(expr: Expr) -> Result<Value, Error> {
    // Specialized, fast path for vector operations
    if matches!(expr, Expr::VecOp) {
        return vec_fast_path();
    }
    // Falls back to interpreter
    interpret_expr(expr)
}
```

