If the OS watch fails, the function logs an error to standard error and initiates a fallback polling loop using a timer instead of a hardware interrupt. It spawns a waker actor, receives a message from it, discards the initial message, and then enters the main loop with a timeout of 100 units.

```fpr
begin me = case OS.watchOpen Unit of
    Err why ->
      _ = Sys.stderr "poller: no watcher ({why}); polling on a timer instead\n";
      loop me 0 0 (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100
  | Ok irq ->
      w = spawn (fn self -> waker me irq self);
      m = receiveFrom me w;
      _ = drop m;
      loop me irq w (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100.
```

The `waking` function implements a loop to handle interrupt signals from the OS watcher. It receives a message from the poller actor, checks if it is a `WakerStopped` signal (indicating the interrupt was unbound), and if so, cleans up the IRQ binding and notifies the poller. Otherwise, it acknowledges the interrupt and resends a `Woke` message to the poller to maintain the active state.

```sol
waking poller irq self =
  m = receive self;
  done = m == 0;
  if done then
    _ = unbind irq;
    _ = A.sendSure self poller WakerStopped;
    Unit
  else
    _ = Sys.irqAck irq;
    _ = A.sendSure self poller Woke;
    waking poller irq self.
```

