The `render` function returns a single string containing the rendered HTML output. It processes Markdown input by splitting it into lines, detecting code fences with backticks, and accumulating code content into a buffer. When a closing code fence is encountered, it joins the buffered lines with newlines, escapes them using the `esc` helper, and wraps the result in `<pre>` tags before clearing the buffer.

```sol
const esc = s => s.replace(/&/g,'&amp;').replace(/</g,'&lt;').replace(/>/g,'&gt;');
function render(md) \{
  const out = []; let code = false, buf = [], list = false;
  const flushList = () => \{ if (list) \{ out.push('</ul>'); list = false; } \};
  for (const raw of md.split('\\n')) \{
    if (raw.startsWith('```')) \{
      if (code) \{ out.push('<pre>' + esc(buf.join('\\n')) + '</pre>'); buf = []; }
      code = !code; continue;
    }
    if (code) \{ buf.push(raw); continue; }
    // ... rest of processing
  }
  return out.join('');
}
```

