Atomics require naturally aligned eight-byte locations in ordinary RAM because the underlying hardware instructions, such as `amoswap.d.aqrl` for exchange, rely on this alignment to function correctly. While the documentation notes that arbitrary MMIO does not support atomic operations, ordinary RAM does, provided the eight-byte alignment constraint is met for these specific instructions.

```sol
// Example of an atomic exchange on a naturally aligned 8-byte location
@align 8
var ptr: *u8;
ptr = @amoswap.d.aqrl(ptr, new_value);
```

The exchange operation uses a single `amoswap.d.aqrl` instruction to swap values, whereas compare-exchange implements a retry loop using Load-Link/Store-Conditional (LR/SC) instructions. In the compare-exchange loop, an acquire fence is applied on the read (Load-Link) and a release fence is applied on a successful write (Store-Conditional); if the store fails, an acquire read is performed.

```sol
// Exchange uses a single atomic instruction
amoswap.d.aqrl target, source

// Compare-exchange uses an LR/SC retry loop with fences
loop:
    lr target, value
    sc target, new_value, target
    if (sc == 0) {
        // Success: acquire read happened on lr, release on sc
        return new_value
    }
    // Failure: perform acquire read on next iteration
    goto loop
```

