The `splice` function computes a string representation of the differences between two lists of strings by calculating the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) using two helper functions, `common` and `delta`. It first determines the length of the common prefix using `common`, drops that prefix from both lists, then calculates the length of the common suffix by reversing the remaining parts and applying `common` again. Based on these lengths, it constructs a string containing the prefix, the count of removed items, and the specific inserted items.

```fpr
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]".
```

The `splice` function returns a single String that combines a common prefix, a count of removed items, and a rendered list of inserted items. It calculates the common prefix by comparing the original lists until a mismatch is found, then determines the number of items to remove by subtracting the length of the remaining suffix (found by reversing and matching the tails) from the length of the remaining prefix. The inserted items are taken from the second list after removing the common suffix, and the final result is formatted as a string containing the prefix count, the removal count, and the rendered inserted strings.

```sol
splice old new =
  prefix = common old new 0;
  a = L.drop prefix old;
  b = L.drop prefix new;
  suffix = common (L.reverse a) (L.reverse b) 0;
  remove = L.length a - suffix;
  insert = L.take (L.length b - suffix) b;
  "[{prefix},{remove},{J.render (J.strings insert)}]".
```

