The file `qos/hal/virt/plic.fpr` defines the QEMU virtual PLIC implementation scoped to the wfi-wake design, where the machine takes no traps and MIE remains off. It operates under a claim-then-mask discipline to prevent level-triggered interrupt storms, ensuring that a source is masked upon claim and re-armed only after the actor acknowledges the interrupt. This module functions as a raw library unit exporting symbols for `hal_irq_open`, `hal_irq_claim`, and `hal_irq_ack`, with all accesses being aligned 32-bit loads or stores. The IRQ hart serves as the last live hart, handling all interrupts as auxiliary business distinct from the prime hart.

```fpr
# Example usage pattern in actors.c
hal_irq_open(hart, source);
hal_irq_claim(hart, source);
# ... actor runs ...
hal_irq_ack(hart, source);
```

The drain loop masks the source at the time of claim to prevent re-pending if the signal remains high, and the actor re-arms the source via Sys.irqAck after servicing the device. This ensures exactly-once delivery per acknowledgment.

```sol
# Example of the claim-then-mask pattern logic
hal_irq_claim(source) {
    # Mask the source immediately upon claim to prevent re-pending
    write_plic(source, 0); 
    # ... service the interrupt ...
    # Re-arm the source only after successful servicing
    write_plic(source, 1); 
}
```

These functions serve as raw library units that act as C symbols called by `actors.c` to manage interrupts without allocating memory or performing complex operations. `hal_irq_open` initializes the interrupt source, `hal_irq_claim` masks the source to prevent re-pending storms and delivers the interrupt as a plain `Int` message to the bound actor, and `hal_irq_ack` re-arms the source after the actor services the device. Since the library allocates nothing and every access is a single aligned 32-bit load or store, these functions effectively return control to the caller immediately after performing the necessary atomic memory operation to update the PLIC state.

```fpr
# Example usage pattern in actors.c
hal_irq_open(source_id);
hal_irq_claim(source_id);
# ... actor runs ...
hal_irq_ack(source_id);
```

The application will fail to load or execute correctly because the fixed-slot design relies on a published constant (`QOS_SLOT_BASE`, 0x40000000) rather than extracting an address from a system symbol table. If the host does not agree on this constant, internal references that survive the lack of relocation will point to invalid memory locations, causing a crash. The build tools (`link-qosapp.ld`, `build-portable-app.sh`, and the Makefile) must explicitly agree on this symbol via `--defsym`; otherwise, the link-time isolation breaks and the app cannot find its runtime capability surface.

```sol
// Example of a Sol function attempting to access the fixed slot base
// This will fail if QOS_SLOT_BASE is not defined as 0x40000000 in the host
fn access_fixed_slot() -> u64 {
    return QOS_SLOT_BASE;
}
```

In the `--target=qx64` mode, the three thread-local cells used by hosted lowering (`fpr_posix_hart`, `fpr_x64_a6/a7`) are implemented as plain globals loaded using RIP-relative addressing rather than TLS blocks. This design treats the loaded app as single-hart, where a global is sufficient since there is only one writer, and the fixed-slot image lacks a dynamic loader to register TLS blocks. Consequently, the link-time `@tpoff` constants are invalid for indexing the host's `%fs` TCB.

```sol
// Example: Accessing a TLS cell as a RIP-relative global in QOS-x86_64
// The cell is loaded at a fixed address (e.g., 0x40000000 + offset)
// and accessed via RIP-relative offset at runtime.

func access_tls_cell (addr: u64) {
    // Load the base address of the TLS cell from the static global
    let base = fpr_g_tls_cell_addr; 

    // Access the value using RIP-relative offset (simulated here)
    // In FP-RISC assembly: mov rax, [rip + base_offset]
    let value = load_rip_relative(base);

    return value;
}
```

The `--target=qx64` refinement (`deTlsQosApp`) converts the three thread-local cells (`fpr_posix_hart`, `fpr_x64_a6/a7`) into plain globals loaded RIP-relative. This change is structurally required because a loaded app is single-hart in this design pass (meaning one hart acts as the sole writer, making a global the correct access pattern), and the fixed-slot image lacks a dynamic loader to register a TLS block. Consequently, link-time `@tpoff` constants cannot index the host's `%fs` TCB.

```sol
// Conceptual representation of the lowered thread-local cell access
// Instead of TLS pointer manipulation, the app accesses a global offset.
@global fpr_x64_a6 : u64
@global fpr_x64_a7 : u64

// Example of accessing a value that was previously thread-local
load rip-relative, fpr_x64_a6, dst_reg
```

In the `--target=qx64` design pass, these three thread-local cells become plain globals loaded RIP-relative. This occurs because a loaded app is single-hart (one writer, making a global correct) and a fixed-slot image has no dynamic loader to register a TLS block; consequently, link-time `@tpoff` constants would incorrectly index the host's `%fs` TCB.

```sol
// Example of the resulting global access pattern
fpr_posix_hart := load_rip_relative(&fpr_posix_hart);
fpr_x64_a6 := load_rip_relative(&fpr_x64_a6);
fpr_x64_a7 := load_rip_relative(&fpr_x64_a7);
```

The `fpr_fuel_exhausted` clobber contract is violated because the function is a real C call that clobbers registers a0..a7. The entry stub must spill parameters to frame slots before the check to ensure the contract holds; adding a safepoint elsewhere without this spill or proving no arguments are live will cause incorrect behavior.

```fprisc
// Incorrect: safepoint added without spilling, violating clobber contract
@fpr_fuel_exhausted
  // Safepoint inserted here without prior spill
  check_fuel()
  // ...
```

```fprisc
// Correct: parameters spilled before check
@fpr_fuel_exhausted
  spill_params_to_frame()
  check_fuel()
  // ...
```

