A field maintains a single snapshot of its raw value per type within closures, allowing the runtime to instantly determine if a change occurred and avoiding unnecessary events for unchanged fields. In contrast, `Live.each` maintains a separate record for every element in a list, storing them under a specific marker key in the model's entries store. When updates occur, `Live.each` identifies changed elements using identity checks and writes only those specific records, whereas a field is read and rewritten as a whole unit.

```sol
// Example of a field update (costs nothing if value is same object)
@field x = 10

// Example of Live.each update (only changed entries are written)
@each items = [1, 2, 3]
// If item 2 changes, only the record for item 2 is updated in the store
@each items = [1, 5, 3]
```

The runtime checks if a field changed by comparing the current snapshot against the raw value stored in two closures; if they are the same object, the event costs nothing because no state update is needed. This optimization is further enhanced by `AtMostEvery` windows, which trigger a `SaveDue` immediately after a change within the window rather than waiting for a subsequent unrelated event, preventing data loss during actor kills.

```sol
// Example of a field update that triggers an event only if the value changed
let oldVal = self.field;
if oldVal != newVal {
  self.field = newVal;
  self.triggerEvent();
}
```

While a standard field store holds a single record per field and is rewritten entirely per element at the start of an update, `Live.each` maintains a separate record for each element in the list. It optimizes performance by walking the old and new entries to identify only those that changed using identity checks, then writing updates solely for those specific elements rather than the entire list.

```sol
// Example illustrating the difference in update strategy
// Standard field store: entire list rewritten
// Live.each: only changed elements (id 1 and 4) updated
