Previously, a socket loop running in a single actor pool caused the process heap to grow approximately 175 KB per event because temporaries were retained until a reset. The new approach uses `std.mvu`'s soft death, where each turn sends its state to itself, triggers `Sys.poolReset`, and reads the state back, ensuring turn temporaries die at the boundary. This results in flat constant-state memory (14 MiB steady over 10,000 events) where growth tracks only the receipt log.

