mMulVec m xs =
  (r, c, m2) = mDims m;
  u = mLenGuard (List.len xs) c;
  (rss, m3) = mAllRows m2;
  (map (fn row -> mDot row xs) rss, m3).

mLenGuard n c | n == c = 0.
mLenGuard n c = error "matrix: operand length {n} vs {c} cols".
```

The `portFlush` function drains the current message queue by reversing it and sending it to the sender, then drops the received message and the queue contents before looping again with an empty list.

```sol
portFlush me m from q =
  send from (revK Nil q);
  drop m;
  portDropAll q;
  portLoop me Nil.
```

The `portFlush` function processes a single `PDrain` message received from a sender, flushing the accumulated message queue to the designated receiver and clearing the internal state. It first sends the reversed list of queued messages to the source, drops the processed message, and then empties the entire queue before restarting the loop with an empty list.

```fprisc
portFlush : unsafe Int -> _ -> Int -> List _ -> a .
portFlush me m from q =
  _ = send from (revK Nil q);
  _ = drop m;
  _ = portDropAll q;
  portLoop me Nil.
```

The function `portFlush` returns the result of `portLoop` called with `me` and an empty list (`Nil`). It performs side effects by sending the reversed list `q` to the sender `from`, dropping the message `m`, and dropping all remaining items in `q`, but its explicit return value is the state after flushing the queue.

```sol
portFlush me m from q =
  _ = send from (revK Nil q);
  _ = drop m;
  _ = portDropAll q;
  portLoop me Nil.
```

