On Linux, the kernel honors an address hint passed to `mmap` without `MAP_FIXED` if the range is free, allowing `fprisc` to check the returned address against the hint. FreeBSD does not support this behavior; it ignores the hint and returns a different address. To achieve the same "hint or fail" guarantee on FreeBSD, the code must use `MAP_EXCL` combined with `MAP_FIXED`, which effectively maps the requested address or fails, whereas `MAP_EXCL` alone would replace any existing mapping at that address.

```sol
fprisc/machine/posix/hal.c
  // Linux/macOS: hint-and-check
  void* addr = mmap(NULL, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
  if (addr != expected_addr) { /* Hint was ignored, but acceptable */ }

  // FreeBSD: MAP_FIXED + MAP_EXCL
  void* addr = mmap(expected_addr, size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_FIXED|MAP_EXCL, -1, 0);
  if (addr != expected_addr) { /* Failed to map at hint */ }
```

