To allocate memory, the `alloc` function in `heap.fpr` first checks for overflow, aligns the requested size to a minimum of 96 bytes, and then performs a first-fit search starting from the head of the free list. It uses the `split` function to carve out the requested size from a free block, marks the new block as used, zeros out the payload, and returns the address of the resulting block.

```sol
alloc : Word -> Addr .
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

The `claim` function finalizes a memory block split by setting its usage flag to one, clearing its field and pending flags, initializing its layout descriptor to null, and calculating the remaining capacity. It then zeroes out the payload area, which spans from the block's start address up to the size of the requested unit, ensuring the allocated memory is clean before being returned to the caller.

```fprisc
claim b size =
  _ = split b size;
  _ = Block.setUsed b (w 1);
  _ = Block.setFields b (w 0);
  _ = Block.setPending b (Block.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setLayout b (Desc.null Unit);
  _ = Block.setCap b (Word.sub (Block.size b) (w 64));
  _ = Block.setRc b (w 1);
  p = payload b;
  _ = zeroWords p ((i (Block.size b) - Block.sizeOf Unit) / 8);
  p.
```

The `alloc` function returns a memory address (`Addr`). If the requested `bytes` value is 0, the function calculates a minimum size of 80 bytes (aligning 0 up to 80) and ensures the final size is at least 96 bytes. It then searches for the first suitable block using `firstFit` to claim this size.

```fprisc
alloc bytes =
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 62 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  _ = case Word.bitTest bytes 63 of True -> error "Builtin: allocation overflow" | False -> Unit;
  size0 = Word.add (w 80) (alignUp bytes);
  size = case Word.eq size0 (w 80) of True -> w 96 | False -> size0;
  firstFit (headB Unit) size.
```

