When an actor tries to receive a message from a session that is closed or disconnected, the receive operation fails immediately. This prevents the actor from blocking indefinitely on a message that will never arrive. For example, if actor `a` sends a message to session `b`, and `b` closes its connection, any subsequent call to `b.until(...)` or `b.recv()` will raise an error or return a failure status rather than waiting for a non-existent message.

```python
# Example in FP-RISC/Sol logic
actor a {
  send('msg', 'b');
  // If 'b' is closed here, the send might succeed (depending on implementation details)
  // but any attempt to receive from 'b' will fail:
  receive('from_b'); // This will fail/error if 'b' is closed
}
```

