The memory actor acts as the serialization point for the buddy system to ensure that every memory block request is properly tracked and allocated. While a requester that finds the buddy lock-free serves itself inline, any request where the buddy is held must queue on the actor to prevent race conditions and maintain system consistency. This design ensures that the memory actor manages the pool slabs, message slabs, and other critical structures without holding a spinlock across queued requests, which would cause unnecessary stalls.

```sol
// Example of a requester queuing on the memory actor when the buddy is held
buddy_alloc_try(buddy_ptr) {
    if (buddy_ptr is free) {
        // Inline allocation
        store(acb, buddy_ptr)
        wake()
    } else {
        // Queue on the memory actor (hart 0)
        queue_on(memory_actor)
    }
}
```

