To trigger multiple software interrupts, a developer uses the `fire` function, which resets the interrupt flag to 0, sets the machine interrupt cause register (address 0x80000000) to 1, waits for the flag to be cleared by the CPU, and then recursively calls itself with the next iteration count. The verification is performed by checking that the sequence of resumed program counters matches the expected values `[n, n + 1]`.

```fprisc
fire n = case n == 0 of
  True -> Unit
| False ->
  flag = Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 2164260864);
  _ = Mem.writeWord flag (Word.fromInt 0);
  _ = Mem.write32 (Addr.fromWord (Word.fromInt 33554432)) (Word.fromInt 1);
  _ = waitFor flag;
  xs = [n, n + 1];
  _ = check (xs == [n, n + 1]);
  fire (n - 1).
```

