The code fails because `Json.render` returns a `String`, and printing it directly consumes the value without preserving it for the subsequent `Json.parse` operation. In FP-RISC, you must explicitly bind the rendered string to a variable before parsing it again. The fix is to assign the result of `Json.render big` to a variable (e.g., `t_str`) and then parse that variable:

```sol
big = Json.Sequence (List.map (fn i -> Json.object (("id", Json.Integer i) :: ("name", Json.Text "item {i}") :: Nil)) (List.range 1 20000));
t_str = Json.render big;
t = t_str;
print "big {String.length t} bytes, parses back equal: {Json.parse t == Ok big}".
```

You parse the JSON string using `Json.parse`, which returns a `Result` type containing either an error or a parsed value. To extract specific fields or nested structures, you use `Json.field` for top-level keys and `Json.at` for deep paths, often chaining them with `Option.andThen` to safely convert types. If a field is missing, `Json.field` returns `None`, which can be handled by printing a message or skipping the value.

```fprisc
main =
  text = "{\"name\": \"fpr\", \"nested\": {\"deep\": {\"x\": -7}}}";
  _ = (case Json.parse text of
      Err e -> print "ERR {e}"
    | Ok v ->
        _ = print "port {Json.field \"port\" v |> Option.andThen Json.asInt}";
        _ = print "deep {Json.at (\"nested\" :: \"deep\" :: \"x\" :: Nil) v}";
  _);
```

