To perform one epoch, you iterate through the dataset using `Vec.fold` to compute scores for weights and biases, then update parameters using the learning rate and L2 lambda. The process involves folding over the input vectors to aggregate gradients, applying the update rule `parameter = parameter - lr * (gradient/n + lam * parameter)`, and returning the new parameters and the accumulated value.

```sol
epoch n lr w1 w2 b v =
  (s1, v1) = Vec.fold (hW1 w1 w2 b) 0 v;
  (s2, v2) = Vec.fold (hW2 w1 w2 b) 0 v1;
  (s3, v3) = Vec.fold (hB w1 w2 b) 0 v2;
  w1n = w1 - lr * (s1 / n + lam * w1);
  w2n = w2 - lr * (s2 / n + lam * w2);
  (w1n, w2n, b - lr * (s3 / n), v3).
```

The `epoch` function implements stochastic gradient descent with L2 regularization by first folding the input vector through three hidden functions (`hW1`, `hW2`, `hB`) to compute scores and accumulate values. It then calculates the new weights and bias by subtracting the learning rate multiplied by the average gradient (score divided by the number of samples) plus the regularization term (lambda times the weight). The specific update formulas are: `w1n = w1 - lr * (s1 / n + lam * w1)`, `w2n = w2 - lr * (s2 / n + lam * w2)`, and `b = b - lr * (s3 / n)`.

```sol
w1n = w1 - lr * (s1 / n + lam * w1);
w2n = w2 - lr * (s2 / n + lam * w2);
(b - lr * (s3 / n), v3).
```

