To configure the UART baud rate divisor, you must call the `doParams` function while owning the device. The function takes the message handle, the current state, the source message handle, and the divisor value `d`. It sets the Divisor Latch Control (DLC) bit to 1 to enable the 16-bit divisor mode, writes the low byte of the divisor to register 0, writes the high byte to register 1, and finally disables the DLC bit to restore 8N1 settings.

```fpr
doParams me st from d =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = write (reg8 dev 3) 131;
  _ = write (reg8 dev 0) (band d 255);
  _ = write (reg8 dev 1) (BITSHIFTR d 8);
  _ = write (reg8 dev 3) 3;
  reply me from UOk st.
```

To configure the UART baud rate, you must first enable the Divisor Latch Access Bit (DLAB) by writing 131 (0x83) to the control register, then write the lower and upper bytes of the divisor to the data registers, and finally restore the control register to 3 (0x03) to disable DLAB. The divisor value `d` is split into the lower byte (`d AND 255`) and the upper byte (`d shifted left by 8`).

```fpr
doParams2 me st from d =
  St dev owner cap txq rxs sub fl armed = st;
  _ = write (reg8 dev 3) 131;
  _ = write (reg8 dev 0) (band d 255);
  _ = write (reg8 dev 1) (BITSHIFTR d 8);
  _ = write (reg8 dev 3) 3;
  reply me from UOk st.
```

The QOS operating system enforces strict ownership control for the UART device using a state field (`owner`) to prevent concurrent access. The `doOpen` function checks if the device is claimed; if so, it returns an error, otherwise it proceeds to initialize the device. The `doParams` function allows only the current owner to modify baud rates by writing to the divisor latch register (DLAB set to 1), the divisor low register (DLL), the divisor high register (DLM), and finally resetting DLAB. This sequence ensures that baud rate changes are applied correctly without interfering with the FIFO status register.

```sol
// Open the UART device
doOpen me st from 0;

// Configure baud rate 9600 (0x0300 for 115200/128 or similar divisor logic)
// Assuming 'd' holds the calculated divisor value
doParams me st from 0 d;
```

