To initialize a poller, call the `begin` function with an unsafe integer representing the interrupt number. If the OS watch operation fails, the function logs an error to standard error and automatically switches to polling via a timer loop. If successful, it spawns a waker actor and enters a main loop.

```fpr
begin me = case OS.watchOpen Unit of
    Err why ->
      _ = Sys.stderr "poller: no watcher ({why}); polling on a timer instead\n";
      loop me 0 0 (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100
  | Ok irq ->
      w = spawn (fn self -> waker me irq self);
      m = receiveFrom me w;
      _ = drop m;
      loop me irq w (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100.
```

If `OS.watchOpen` fails, the `begin` function logs an error to standard stderr and initiates polling on a timer instead of an interrupt. It spawns a `waker` actor, receives a dummy message, and immediately enters the `loop` function to manage the timer-based polling state.

```fpr
begin me = case OS.watchOpen Unit of
    Err why ->
      _ = Sys.stderr "poller: no watcher ({why}); polling on a timer instead\n";
      loop me 0 0 (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100
  | Ok irq ->
      w = spawn (fn self -> waker me irq self);
      m = receiveFrom me w;
      _ = drop m;
      loop me irq w (M.empty Ord.int) A.tidy0 100.
```

