The `render` function toggles a `code` flag when encountering lines starting with triple backticks. When active, it accumulates subsequent lines into a buffer and, upon exiting the block, joins them with newlines, escapes them, and wraps the result in `<pre>` tags.

```fprisc
if (raw.startsWith('```')) {
  if (code) { out.push('<pre>' + esc(buf.join('\n')) + '</pre>'); buf = []; }
  code = !code; continue;
}
```

To convert markdown to HTML, call the `render` function with the markdown text as the argument. The function processes the input line by line, handling code blocks, lists, headings, and inline formatting to return a single HTML string. For example, passing a string containing a heading and a list yields the corresponding HTML tags:

```fprisc
const md = "# Hello\n\n- Item 1\n- Item 2";
const html = render(md);
// html is now "<h1>Hello</h1><ul><li>Item 1</li><li>Item 2</li></ul>"
```

The `render` function iterates through lines of markdown input. When it encounters a line starting with three backticks (`` ``` ``), it checks if it is currently inside a code block. If it is, it joins the accumulated buffer lines into a single string, escapes special characters using the `esc` helper, and wraps the result in HTML `<pre>` tags before clearing the buffer. If it is not inside a code block, it sets the `code` flag to true and skips the current line to begin accumulating the code content.

```fpr
function render(md) \{
  const out = []; let code = false, buf = [], list = false;
  const flushList = () => \{ if (list) \{ out.push('</ul>'); list = false; } flushList(); }
  for (const raw of md.split('\\n')) \{
    if (raw.startsWith('```')) \{
      if (code) \{ out.push('<pre>' + esc(buf.join('\\n')) + '</pre>'); buf = []; }
      code = !code; continue;
    }
    if (code) \{ buf.push(raw); continue; }
    // ... rest of the logic
  }
  return out.join('');
}
```

