The `machine/unix` backend enables real hardware discovery and booting via the OpenSBI path, whereas `machine/virt` relies on simulated devices. Specifically, `machine/unix` parses the Device Tree Blob (DTB) at boot to dynamically determine memory size and hart count, enabling "unbounded memory," while `machine/virt` uses a fixed constant. Additionally, `machine/unix` accesses physical peripherals like SD/MMC controllers and real NICs, and retrieves actual PLIC/CLINT addresses from the DTB, whereas `machine/virt` uses simulated virtio-blk and virtio-net devices.

```sol
