To find the bounding box, you first extract the x and y coordinates from the list of points, compute the minimum and maximum for each axis, and then apply a padding function if the range is zero. The `padB` function adjusts the bounds by subtracting 1 from the lower limit and adding 1 to the upper limit only when the difference between the high and low values is exactly 0. This process is encapsulated in the `ptsBounds` function, which returns a tuple of `(minx, maxx, miny, maxy)`.

```sol
ptsBounds pts =
  xs = map (fn p -> fst2 p) pts;
  ys = map (fn p -> snd2 p) pts;
  (lo1, hi1) = padB (bMin xs) (bMax xs);
  (lo2, hi2) = padB (bMin ys) (bMax ys);
  (lo1, hi1, lo2, hi2).

padB lo hi = case hi - lo == 0 of True -> (lo - 1, hi + 1) | False -> (lo, hi).
```

