The dataset is generated by defining a `mkRow` function that creates instances using `Rand.next` and `Rand.gauss4` to produce continuous features `x1` and `x2`. The true label `ytrue` is determined by a nested axis-aligned rule: `y = +1` if `x1 > 0.25` and `x2 > -0.5`, otherwise `-1`. A 5% label noise is introduced by flipping the label based on a uniform random check against 20, while the final row includes these modifications. The `fill` function iteratively applies `mkRow` to construct the full vector of data points.

```sol
mkRow s =
  sa = Rand.next s;
  x1 = Rand.gauss4 sa;
  sb = Rand.next4 sa;
  x2 = Rand.gauss4 sb;
  sc = Rand.next4 sb;
  flip = Rand.uniform sc 20;
  ytrue = case x1 > quarter of
    False -> 0 - 1
  | True -> (case x2 > minusHalf of True -> 1 | False -> 0 - 1);
  {x1 = x1, x2 = x2, y = case flip == 0 of True -> 0 - ytrue | False -> ytrue}.
```

The portable backend, running within the QOS operating system, lacks hardware timers and instead spawns sleeper child processes using Sys.sleepUs to simulate waiting periods. In contrast, the native backend on bare metal utilizes the CLINT hardware timer to arm a deadline interrupt, which the MTIP pops out of the WFI state to deliver the message directly.

```fpr
# Portable simulation using Sys.sleepUs
_ = T.sleep svc 50; 

# Native simulation using CLINT hardware timer
t0 = read clk;
_ = T.sleep svc 50;
d1 = read clk - t0;
```

To verify that a blocking sleep in QOS mode waited for the requested duration, you must read the CLINT clock register before and after the sleep call and compare the difference against the requested time in nanoseconds. In QOS mode, the hardware timer is unavailable, so the system spawns sleeper children that wait for `Sys.sleepUs`; the test confirms the wait by checking if the elapsed clock time is at least 500,000 nanoseconds (50 ms).

```fpr
t0 = read clk;
_ = T.sleep svc 50;
d1 = read clk - t0;
s50 = case d1 >= 500000 of True -> "ok" | False -> "SHORT({d1})";
```

The `T.after` function schedules a fire-and-forget deadline that is delivered to the caller's own mailbox after a specified delay, rather than interrupting the current execution context. When multiple deadlines are pending, the system ensures they fire through a single armed slot in the order of their proximity, guaranteeing that the nearest deadline is processed first.

```fpr
main =
  me = myself 0;
  svc = spawn TM.actor;

  # Schedule two deadlines: 20ms and 40ms
  _ = T.after svc 20;
  _ = T.after svc 40;

  # Collect the nearest deadline (20ms)
  f = receiveFrom me svc;
  case f of TFire -> "nearest_fired" | o -> "error";
```

The `goalMet` function evaluates a composite goal tree by recursively checking if all child nodes are satisfied. In a composite tree defined with `done = { none | over = 1 }`, the system first checks the immediate child (`none`). If that child's condition is met, the `over` flag increments, and the composite node is considered complete only if all children have been processed. For example, `R.goalMet done R.goal0` returns true only after every node in the tree, including those marked with `over = 1`, has successfully satisfied its requirements.

```sol
done = { none | over = 1 };
_ = expectT "reaching it completes the tree" (R.goalMet done R.goal0);
```

