When filtering a vector of records, FP-RISC performs compaction over the columns, effectively removing elements that do not satisfy the predicate while maintaining the SoA (Structure of Arrays) layout. Subsequent operations like `map` and `fold` operate on this compacted vector. For instance, filtering a vector of 100 records where the x-field exceeds 90, mapping to increment the y-field, and then folding with a sum operation results in a sum of 2085 and a final length of 10.

```fprisc
j1 = fillR (Vec.new Unit) 1 100;
j2 = Vec.filter (fn p -> p.x > 90) j1;
j3 = Vec.map (fn p -> {p | y = p.y + 1}) j2;
(js, j4) = Vec.fold sumR 0 j3;
(jn, j5) = Vec.len j4;
verdict "test" (js == 2085 && jn == 10);
Vec.free j5;
```

