In FP-RISC's Sol scripting profile, mutating operations like file writing and directory creation are buffered and applied atomically at the end of the script. Instead of executing immediately, these calls queue for a single commit, ensuring that either all changes succeed or none are persisted. To implement this, use the `writePath` function to create or overwrite files, and `mkdirp` to create directories with their parents, both within a script that completes before any disk I/O occurs.

```sol
scaffold root =
  u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
  u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
  u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n\ngenerated by sol\n";
  u3 = writePath "{root}/src/main.sol" "> print \"hello from {base.baseName root}\".\n";
  print "scaffolded {root} (4 files, 2 dirs) — atomic".
```

To achieve atomic directory creation and file population without leaving partial changes, a Sol script must use the `mkdirp` and `writePath` tool calls, which buffer all mutating operations until the script completes successfully. This approach ensures that if the script fails, no files are touched, and if it succeeds, all files and directories are created or overwritten as a single transaction. For example, a script can define a sequence of operations to create parent directories and write files, printing a confirmation only after the entire pipeline validates:

```sol
scaffold root =
  u0 = mkdirp "{root}/src";
  u1 = mkdirp "{root}/test";
  u2 = writePath "{root}/README.md" "# {base.baseName root}\n\ngenerated by sol\n";
  u3 = writePath "{root}/src/main.sol" "> print \"hello from {base.baseName root}\".\n";
  u4 = writePath "{root}/test/basic.sol" "> print \"test stub\".\n";
  print "scaffolded {root} (4 files, 2 dirs) — atomic".
```

