The ARC release mechanism prevents cycles and ensures correct deallocation by decrementing a block's reference count before queuing it for processing. If the count reaches zero, the block is pushed onto a worklist for immediate field traversal and memory deallocation. The code explicitly checks for a zero count to determine whether to continue the queue or push the block for processing:

```fprisc
enqueueLive b work =
  _ = case Word.eq (Block.rc b) (w 0) of True -> error "Builtin: duplicate ownership or cycle" | False -> Unit;
  left = Word.sub (Block.rc b) (w 1);
  _ = Block.setRc b left;
  case Word.eq left (w 0) of
    True -> push b work
  | False -> work.
```

