When an actor runs out of fuel, it must yield control back to the scheduler. The actor system relies on this fuel mechanism to enforce cooperative yielding, ensuring that the scheduler can reclaim the actor's resources and manage execution flow.

```fprisc
actor main() {
  let fuel = 1;
  while (fuel > 0) {
    fuel = fuel - 1;
    // If fuel becomes 0 here, the actor must yield control to the scheduler
    // before executing any further logic.
  }
}
```

Fuel is a resource consumed by each actor during execution to enable cooperative yielding. When an actor's fuel runs out, it must voluntarily yield control back to the scheduler.

```fprisc
actor main {
    let fuel = 10;
    loop {
        if (fuel <= 0) {
            yield(); // Must yield when fuel is exhausted
        } else {
            fuel = fuel - 1;
        }
    }
}
```

The system uses a surfaceless EGL context to render an ES 3.1 scene into an offscreen framebuffer object (FBO). The `drm_scanout.h` module then transfers this frame to the monitor via a KMS dumb buffer by issuing a single `SetCrtc` call to the first available `/dev/dri/card*` device with a connected connector.

```sol
// Example Sol code representing the surfaceless rendering and scanout logic
// (Conceptual representation of the flow described in hal/unix/gfx.c and drm_scanout.h)

// 1. Create surfaceless EGL context
ctx = create_egl_context(surfaceless = true)

// 2. Render ES 3.1 scene to offscreen FBO
fbo = create_offscreen_fbo()
render_scene(ctx, target = fbo)

// 3. Scanout to monitor via DRM
