The FPRC compiler computes the WCET as the maximum number of IR instructions between consecutive safepoint events (such as function entry checks or calls) by traversing the emitted assembly as a Control Flow Graph (CFG). Since all loops are implemented as tail calls forming a DAG, the compiler detects back-edges and reports them as UNBOUNDED, ensuring the model relies on finite path lengths. However, the model assumes a fuel unit corresponds to one function entry, which means bodies containing long straight-line vector kernels or large literal folds can still be arbitrarily long. To handle this, kernels must define per-invocation lane caps or decrement fuel per N lanes, where N is chosen so that N times the lane cost does not exceed the hardware timing target.

```fprc
// Example of a WCET comment generated by FPRC
// # wcet: segmax=221, exit-tail=5, ccall=10
// This indicates the maximum IR instructions between safepoints for this segment.
```

The FPRC compiler computes the maximum IR-instruction count on any path between consecutive safepoint events (such as entry checks, kernel checks, or calls) by treating the emitted assembly as a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG), since all loops are implemented as tail calls. It assigns a `# wcet:` comment to each function, which includes the segment maximum, exit-tail count for chaining, and external call count. These counts are determined before lowering and do not yet include target-specific lowering factors or cycles-per-instruction values.

```fprc
